window.AUTH=window.AUTH||{};window.AUTH["22-800"]={"d":"22-800","id":"2023/22-800","n":"Moore v. United States","briefs":[{"k":"petitioner","label":"Brief of petitioners Charles G. Moore, et ux.","date":"2023-08-30","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/22/22-800/278464/20230830102536217_22-800%20Brief%20for%20Petitioners.pdf","side":"pet","n":86},{"k":"respondent","label":"Brief of respondent United States","date":"2023-10-16","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/22/22-800/285200/20231016195041390_22-800bsUnited%20States.pdf","side":"res","n":109},{"k":"reply","label":"Reply of petitioners Charles G. Moore, et ux.","date":"2023-11-15","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/22/22-800/289622/20231115114606125_22-800%20Reply%20Brief.pdf","side":"pet","n":32}],"opinion":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/23pdf/602us1r41_h3dj.pdf","tr":true,"items":[{"t":"case","label":"Barclay & Co. v. Edwards","cite":"267 U.S. 442","y":1925,"id":"scdb/1924-025","sc":1,"sum":"1. The taxation by Congress of the income of domestic corporations derived from sale abroad of goods bought or made by them in this countryr is not a tax on exports; nor does it violate due process of law because a like tax is not imposed on the income similarly derived by foreign corporations. National ]Paper Co. v. Bowers, 266 U. S. 373.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"51","1":"28"}},{"t":"case","label":"Bromley v. McCaughn","cite":"280 U.S. 124","y":1929,"id":"scdb/1929-012","sc":1,"sum":". Mr. Ira Jewell Williams, with whom Messrs. Ira Jewell Williams, Jr., and Francis Skunk Brown were on the brief, for Bromley.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"52"}},{"t":"case","label":"Brushaber v. Union Pac. R.R. Co.","cite":"240 U.S. 1","y":1916,"id":"scdb/1915-067","sc":1,"op":12,"ar":1,"sum":"Under proper averments a stockholder’s suit to restrain, a corporation from voluntarily paying a tax charged to be unconstitutional, is not violative of Rev. Stat., § 3224, and the District Court has jurisdiction to entertain the action. Pollock v. Farmers’ Loan & Trust Co., 157 U. S. 429.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"8, 18, 19, 34, 35","1":"3, 18, 35","2":"6, 15"}},{"t":"case","label":"Burk-Waggoner Oil Ass’n v. Hopkins","cite":"269 U.S. 110","y":1925,"id":"scdb/1925-004","sc":1,"op":8,"sum":"1. Unincorporated joint stock associations, like those described in Hecht v. Malley, 265 U. S. 144, though partnerships under the state law, are \" corporations,\" within the definition of the Revenue Act of 1918, and are subject, like corporations, to the income and excess profits taxes imposed by that Act.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"35","1":"41"}},{"t":"case","label":"Chiafalo v. Washington","cite":"140 S. Ct. 2316","y":2020,"id":"2019/19-465","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"The Court held that the Constitution does not prohibit Washington's “faithless elector” law, which requires presidential electors to vote the way state law directs.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"26"}},{"t":"case","label":"Comm’r v. Banks","cite":"543 U.S. 426","y":2005,"id":"2004/03-892","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"In a unanimous, 8-0 opinion delivered by Justice Anthony Kennedy, the Court held that if a litigant's recovery constituted income, the litigant's income included the contingency fee paid to his or her lawyer. The Court held that an economic gain assigned in advance to another party - as in a contingency-fee agreement - could not be excluded from gross income.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"36","1":"13, 14, 33, 41","2":"10"}},{"t":"case","label":"Comm’r v. Culbertson","cite":"337 U.S. 733","y":1949,"id":"scdb/1948-107","sc":1,"sum":"Respondent taxpayer, engaged in the business of breeding and selling cattle, formed a family partnership with his four sons, to whom he sold an undivided one-half interest in the business, taking their promissory note therefc:. The note was paid by proceeds from the business and by gifts from respondent. The eldest son was, before and after the formation of the partnership, foreman of the ranch and received …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"28"}},{"t":"case","label":"Comm’r v. Glenshaw Glass Co.","cite":"348 U.S. 426","y":1955,"id":"scdb/1954-042","sc":1,"op":2,"sum":"Money received as exemplary damages for fraud or as the punitive two-thirds portion of a treble-damage antitrust recovery must be reported by a taxpayer as \"gross income\" under § 22 (a) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1939.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"24, 25, 28, 43","1":"12, 27, 38, 41","2":"4, 7, 8"}},{"t":"case","label":"Comm’r v. Indianapolis Power & Light Co.","cite":"493 U.S. 203","y":1990,"id":"1989/88-1319","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"The customer deposits are not advance payments for electricity and therefore do not constitute taxable income to IPL upon receipt. Although IPL derives some economic benefit from the deposits, it does not have the requisite \"complete dominion\" over them at the time they are made, the crucial point for determining taxable income. IPL has an obligation to repay the deposits upon termination of service or satisfaction …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"25, 36","1":"38","2":"8"}},{"t":"case","label":"Comm’r v. Kowalski","cite":"434 U.S. 77","y":1977,"id":"1977/76-1095","sc":1,"op":2,"sum":"New Jersey provides a cash meal allowance for its state police troopers, which is paid biweekly in advance in an amount varying with the trooper's rank and is included, although separately stated, with his salary and in his gross pay for purposes of calculating pension benefits. Although troopers are required to remain on call in their assigned patrol areas during their midshift break, they are not required to eat …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"25","1":"39"}},{"t":"case","label":"Connecticut Gen. Life Ins. Co. v. Eaton","cite":"218 F. 188","y":1914,"court":"D. Conn.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F./218/188/","op":1,"ar":1,"by":{"0":"28"}},{"t":"case","label":"Corliss v. Bowers","cite":"281 U.S. 376","y":1930,"id":"scdb/1929-053","sc":1,"op":1,"ar":1,"sum":"1. Taxation is not so much concerned with the refinements of titlc as it is with actual command over the property taxed, the actual benefit for which the tax is paid.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"24, 48"}},{"t":"case","label":"Cullinan v. Walker","cite":"262 U.S. 134","y":1923,"id":"scdb/1922-170","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"1. The gain accruing to a shareholder through enhancement of the value of his shares, and which, when segregated, becomes legally income subject to the income tax, may be segregated by a dividend made on liquidation of the corporation as well as by an ordinary dividend.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"20"}},{"t":"case","label":"Diedrich v. Comm’r","cite":"457 U.S. 191","y":1982,"id":"1981/80-2204","sc":1,"op":2,"sum":"A donor (such as petitioner husband and wife and petitioner executor's decedent) who makes a gift of property on condition that the donee pay the resulting gift taxes realizes taxable income to the extent that the gift taxes paid by the donee exceed the donor's adjusted basis in the property.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"47"}},{"t":"case","label":"Dobbs v. Jackson Women’s Health Org.","cite":"142 S. Ct. 2228","y":2022,"id":"2021/19-1392","sc":1,"sum":"The Constitution does not confer a right to abortion; Roe v. Wade, 410 U. S. 113, and Planned Parenthood of Southeastern Pa. v. Casey, 505 U. S. 833, are overruled; the authority to regulate abortion is returned to the people and their elected representatives.","sumSrc":"Holding (supremecourt.gov)","by":{"0":"36"}},{"t":"case","label":"Dougherty v. Comm’r","cite":"60 T.C. 917","y":1973,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/T.C./60/917/","sum":"Upholding Subpart F’s application to a shareholder’s portion of a CFC’s accumulated “income of a past year prior to the effective date of the statute”.","sumSrc":"brief:1","by":{"0":"51","1":"27, 44"}},{"t":"case","label":"Doyle v. Mitchell Bros. Co.","cite":"247 U.S. 179","y":1918,"id":"scdb/1917-168","sc":1,"sum":"The purpose of the Corporation Tax Act of August 5, 1909, c. 6, 36 Stat. 11, 112, § 38, is not to tax property as such, or the mere conversion of property, but to tax the conduct of the business of corporations organized for profit by a measure based upon the gainful returns from their business operations and property from the time the act took effect.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"28"}},{"t":"case","label":"Eisner v. Macomber","cite":"252 U.S. 189","y":1920,"id":"scdb/1919-124","sc":1,"op":16,"sum":"Congress was not empowered by the Sixteenth Amendment to tax, as income of the stockholder, without apportionment, a stock dividend made lawfully and in' good faith against profits accumulated by the corporation since March 1,1913. P. 201. Toume v. Mmer, 245 U. S. 418.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"1, 2, 13–15, 17–27, 33, 35–43, 47, 48, 50","1":"10, 11, 33-36","2":"2-10, 16, 18, 19, 21, 22, 25, 26"}},{"t":"case","label":"Flint v. Stone Tracy Co.","cite":"220 U.S. 107","y":1911,"id":"scdb/1910-091","sc":1,"op":1,"ar":1,"sum":"APPEAL FROM THE CIRCUIT COURT OF THE UNITED STATES FOR THE SOUTHERN DISTRICT OF NEW YORK.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"53","1":"11, 47, 48","2":"16, 22, 25"}},{"t":"case","label":"Garlin v. Murphy","cite":"42 A.D.2d 30","y":1973,"court":"N.Y. App. Div. 3d","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/A.D.2d/42/30/","by":{"0":"52"}},{"t":"case","label":"Garlock Inc. v. Comm’r","cite":"58 T.C. 423","y":1972,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/T.C./58/423/","sum":"Finding “constructive receipt of income” by taxpayer.","sumSrc":"brief:0","by":{"0":"51"}},{"t":"case","label":"Gibbons v. Mahon","cite":"136 U.S. 549","y":1890,"id":"scdb/1889-287","sc":1,"op":1,"ar":1,"sum":"— Decided May 19, 1890.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"28","2":"18, 23"}},{"t":"case","label":"Goesele v. Bimeler","cite":"55 U.S. 589","y":1852,"id":"scdb/1852-056","sc":1,"sum":"A society called Separatists, emigrated from Germany to tlie United States. They were very poor, and one of them, in 1817, purchased land in Ohio, for which he gave his bond, and took the title to himself. Afterwards, they adopted two constitutions, one in 1816, and one in 1824, which they signed, and in 1832 obtained an act of incorporation. The articles of association, or constitutions of 1819 and 1824, contained …","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"41"}},{"t":"case","label":"Gray v. Darlington","cite":"82 U.S. 63","y":1872,"id":"scdb/1872-024","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"An act of Congress of March 2d, 1867, provides that","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"27"}},{"t":"case","label":"Haynes v. United States","cite":"390 U.S. 85","y":1968,"id":"1967/236","sc":1,"sum":"Petitioner was charged by information with violating 26 U. S. C. § 5851 (part of the National Firearms Act, an interrelated statutory system for the taxation of certain classes of firearms used principally by persons engaged in unlawful activities) by knowingly possessing a defined firearm which had not been registered as required by 26 U. S. C. § 5841. Section 5841 obligates the possessor of a defined firearm to …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"51"}},{"t":"case","label":"Heiner v. Mellon","cite":"304 U.S. 271","y":1938,"id":"scdb/1937-080","sc":1,"op":19,"ar":7,"sum":"Assistant Solicitor General Bell, with whom Solicitor General Reed, Assistant Attorney General Morris, Mr. J. Louis Monarch, Helen R. Carloss, and Mr. George H. Zeutzius were on the brief, for petitioner.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"41","1":"24, 42"}},{"t":"case","label":"Helvering v. Bruun","cite":"309 U.S. 461","y":1940,"id":"scdb/1939-110","sc":1,"op":5,"sum":"1. Where, upon termination of a lease, the lessor repossessed the real estate and improvements, including a new building erected by the lessee, an increase in value attributable to the new building was taxable under the Revenue Act of 1932 as income of the lessor in the year of repossession. P. 467. Argument for Respondent.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"23, 42","1":"36, 37","2":"6, 7"}},{"t":"case","label":"Helvering v. Eubank","cite":"311 U.S. 122","y":1940,"id":"scdb/1940-012","sc":1,"op":2,"sum":"Renewal commissions paid in 1933 by insurance companies to the assignee of an agent, pursuant to assignments made by the agent, in 1924 and 1928, of'such commissions as should become payable to him for services which had been rendered in writing policies of insurance under agency contracts, held, under § 22 of the Revenue Act of 1932, income taxable in 1933 to the assignor. Following Helvering v. Horst, ante, p. …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"24"}},{"t":"case","label":"Helvering v. Gowran","cite":"302 U.S. 238","y":1937,"id":"scdb/1937-030","sc":1,"sum":"(1) That the whole of the proceeds of the sale were taxable as income. P. 243. The computation is under §§ 111 and 113, Revenue Act of 1928, which provide that the gain from conversion of property into money shall be computed at the excess of the amount realized over the \"cost\" of the property, which in this case was zero. (2) The stock dividend was not to be likened to gifts and legacies, as to which there are …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"21","1":"36"}},{"t":"case","label":"Helvering v. Griffiths","cite":"318 U.S. 371","y":1943,"id":"scdb/1942-095","sc":1,"op":5,"sum":"A holder of common stock in a corporation which had but the one class of stock outstanding received, in 1939, stock dividends (based on earnings and profits subsequent to February 28, 1913) in common stock identical with the stock on which they were declared. The dividend stock was in no way realized upon in 1939. Held, upon consideration of the legislative history and administrative construction, that Congress, by …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"22, 39, 43","1":"18, 35, 37","2":"6, 7"}},{"t":"case","label":"Helvering v. Horst","cite":"311 U.S. 112","y":1940,"id":"scdb/1940-027","sc":1,"op":3,"sum":"1. Where, in 1934 and 1935, an owner of negotiable bonds, who reported income on the cash receipts basis, detached from the bonds negotiable interest coupons before their due date and delivered them as a gift to his son, who in the same year collected them at maturity, held that, under § 22 of the Revenue Act of 1934, and in the year that the interest payments were made, there was a realization of income, in the …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"23, 24, 42, 43","1":"8, 37, 39","2":"6"}},{"t":"case","label":"Helvering v. Indep. Life Ins. Co.","cite":"292 U.S. 371","y":1934,"id":"scdb/1933-128","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"1. A federal tax upon part of a building occupied by the owner, or upon the rental value of the space, is a direct tax and invalid unless apportioned.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"35"}},{"t":"case","label":"Helvering v. Sprouse","cite":"318 U.S. 604","y":1943,"id":"scdb/1942-118","sc":1,"op":2,"by":{"0":"22"}},{"t":"case","label":"Ivan Allen Co. v. United States","cite":"422 U.S. 617","y":1975,"id":"1974/74-22","sc":1,"op":5,"ar":2,"sum":"In determining the applicability of § 533 (a) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1954-which provides a rebuttable presumption that a corporation that has accumulated earnings \"beyond the reasonable needs of the business\" did so with \"the purpose to avoid the income tax with respect to shareholders\"--listed and readily marketable securities owned by the corporation and purchased out of its earnings and profits, are to …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"25, 35, 38","2":"25"}},{"t":"case","label":"James v. United States","cite":"366 U.S. 213","y":1961,"id":"1960/63","sc":1,"sum":"The judgment affirming the conviction is reversed and the cause is remanded with directions to dismiss the indictment. Pp. 214-215, 222. 273 F. 2d 5, reversed.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"25","1":"39"}},{"t":"case","label":"Koshland v. Helvering","cite":"298 U.S. 441","y":1936,"id":"scdb/1935-065","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"1. Common voting shares of a corporation received by the holder of cumulative preferred shares as a dividend-held income and not to be ireated as returns of capital. P. 443. Therefore, upon a subsequent sale or other disposition of the preferred shares no part of their original cost is to be apportioned to such common shares for the purpose of determining the gain or loss from such disposition.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"21, 22, 39","1":"36","2":"7"}},{"t":"case","label":"Lynch v. Hornby","cite":"247 U.S. 339","y":1918,"id":"scdb/1917-145","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"The Income Tax Act of October 3, 1913, c. 16, 38 Stat. 166, drew a distinction between a shareholder’s undivided interest in the gains and profits of a corporation prior to declaration of a dividend, a,nd his participation in the dividends declared and paid; treating the latter, in ordinary circumstances, as part of his income for the purpose of the “surtax,” and not regarding the former as taxable to him unless …","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"19","1":"34, 35","2":"18"}},{"t":"case","label":"MacLaughlin v. Alliance Ins. Co. of Philadelphia","cite":"286 U.S. 244","y":1932,"id":"scdb/1931-093","sc":1,"op":1,"ar":1,"sum":"1. While increase in value of property, not realized as gain by its sale or other disposition, may, in an economic or bookkeeping sense, be deemed an addition to capital in a later period, it is nevertheless a gain from capital investment which, when realized by conversion into money or other property, constitutes income within the meaning of the Sixteenth Amendment, taxable as such in the period when realized.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"23"}},{"t":"case","label":"Mallory v. Norfolk S. Ry. Co.","cite":"143 S. Ct. 2028","y":2023,"id":"2022/21-1168","sc":1,"sum":"A Pennsylvania law requiring out-of-state companies that register to do business in Pennsylvania to agree to appear in Pennsylvania courts on “any cause of action” against them comports with the Due Process Clause under Pennsylvania Fire Ins. Co. of Philadelphia v. Gold Issue Mining & Milling Co., 243 U. S. 93 (1917).","sumSrc":"Holding (supremecourt.gov)","by":{"0":"44"}},{"t":"case","label":"Marr v. United States","cite":"268 U.S. 536","y":1925,"id":"scdb/1924-162","sc":1,"sum":"that the new securities thus received by an old stock-holder were not in effect a stock dividend; and that their value above the cost of his exchanged securities, bought by him prior to March 1, 1913, was taxable as income under the Act of September 8, 1916, and within the power of Congress so to tax, since the corporations were essentially different, being organized in different States and with different rights and …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"21"}},{"t":"case","label":"Maryland Cas. Co. v. United States","cite":"52 Ct. Cl. 201","y":1917,"court":"Ct. Cl.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Ct.%20Cl./52/201/","op":1,"by":{"0":"27","1":"20"}},{"t":"case","label":"McDonald v. Maxwell","cite":"274 U.S. 91","y":1927,"id":"scdb/1926-051","sc":1,"by":{"0":"21"}},{"t":"case","label":"M.E. Blatt Co. v. United States","cite":"305 U.S. 267","y":1938,"id":"scdb/1938-013","sc":1,"sum":"erroneous. (1) The question presented is whether, under this particular lease, one-tenth of this \"estimated depreciated value,\" at the end of the term, was income of the lessor in the first year of the term. There is nothing in the findings to suggest that cost of any improvement made by lessee was rent or an expenditure not properly to be attributed to its capital or maintenance account as distinguished from …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"42"}},{"t":"case","label":"Merchants’ Loan & Trust Co. v. Smietanka","cite":"255 U.S. 509","y":1921,"id":"scdb/1920-142","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"1. A provision in a will creating a trust that accretions of selling value shall be considered principal and not income, can not render them non-taxable under the income tax law.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"33","2":"5, 12"}},{"t":"case","label":"Merchants’ Nat’l Bank of Cincinnati v. Wehrmann","cite":"202 U.S. 295","y":1906,"id":"scdb/1905-118","sc":1,"sum":"Where a national bank sued for debts of a partnership, sharés of which it had taken as security and afterwards acquired in payment of the debt, sets up at every stage of the suit its intention of relying on the bankruptcy law of the United States, it cannot be required in the first instance to anticipate the specific and qualified form in which the immunity finally was denied; and if in addition thereto there is a …","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"42","1":"25"}},{"t":"case","label":"Miles v. Safe Deposit & Trust Co. of Baltimore","cite":"259 U.S. 247","y":1922,"id":"scdb/1921-157","sc":1,"sum":"1. A preferential right accorded pro rtita to’the stockholders of á corporation to subscribe at a stated price for a new issue of shares, is not a fruit of stock ownership in the nature of a profit, nor a '- division of any part of the corporate assets.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"21"}},{"t":"case","label":"Minot v. Paine","cite":"99 Mass. 101","y":1868,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Mass./99/101/","by":{"0":"28","1":"20"}},{"t":"case","label":"Murphy v. United States","cite":"992 F.2d 929","y":1993,"court":"9th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/992/929/","by":{"0":"52"}},{"t":"case","label":"Mut. Ben. Life Ins. Co. v. Herold","cite":"198 F. 199","y":1912,"court":"D.N.J.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F./198/199/","by":{"0":"27"}},{"t":"case","label":"Nat’l Fed’n of Indep. Bus. v. Sebelius","cite":"567 U.S. 519","y":2012,"id":"2011/11-393","sc":1,"op":5,"sum":"Cases in which the Court held that the minimum coverage requirement of the Affordable Care Act was constitutional, that Congress had the power to enforce the taxation of most Americans that did not buy the minimum level of coverage, and that the federal government exceeded its power by pressuring States into accepting conditions that Congress could not impose directly by threatening to withhold all federal funding under Medicaid.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"35","1":"3, 4","2":"5"}},{"t":"case","label":"NLRB v. Noel Canning","cite":"573 U.S. 513","y":2014,"id":"2013/12-1281","sc":1,"sum":"A case in which the Court further defined the limitations on the presidential powers granted by the Recess Appointments Clause.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"37, 47","1":"22, 26"}},{"t":"case","label":"Pollock v. Farmers’ Loan & Trust Co.","cite":"157 U.S. 429","y":1895,"id":"1850-1900/157us429","sc":1,"op":3,"sum":"The Court held that the Income Tax Act of 1894 violated Article I, Section 9, of the Constitution because it was a direct taxation scheme not apportioned among the states.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"1, 5, 8, 19, 27, 34, 35"}},{"t":"case","label":"Rockefeller v. United States","cite":"257 U.S. 176","y":1921,"id":"scdb/1921-023","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"1. Where the stockholders of a corporation, which is engaged in producing, buying and selling crude petroleum and in transporting it through its pipe lines, form a new corporation to which the pipe line property is conveyed by the old corporation and in consideration therefor and as part of the transaction all the capital stock of the new corporation, of par value equal to the valuation of the property so conveyed, …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"20"}},{"t":"case","label":"Ross v. Comm’r","cite":"169 F.2d 483","y":1948,"court":"1st Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/169/483/","by":{"0":"48"}},{"t":"case","label":"S. Pac. Co. v. Lowe","cite":"247 U.S. 330","y":1918,"id":"scdb/1917-144","sc":1,"sum":"Accumulations that accrued to a corporation through surplus earnings or appreciation in property value, before the adoption of the Sixteenth Amendment (February, 1913), and the effective date (March, 1913), of the Income Tax Act of 1913 (Act October 3,1913, c. 16, 38 Stat. 166),.are to be regarded as its capital, not as its income for the purposes of that act.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"48","2":"9"}},{"t":"case","label":"Stratton’s Indep., Ltd. v. Howbert","cite":"231 U.S. 399","y":1913,"id":"scdb/1913-043","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"The Corporation Tax Law of August 5, 1909, e. 6, 36 Stat. 11, 112, applies to mining corporations.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"28","1":"47"}},{"t":"case","label":"Taft v. Bowers","cite":"278 U.S. 470","y":1929,"id":"scdb/1928-028","sc":1,"op":5,"ar":1,"sum":"1. Under par. (2), § 202 of the Revenue Act of 1921, where one who purchased shares of stock after February 28, 1913, gave them to another after December 31, 1.920, when their market value Lad increased over the investment, and the donee afterwards sold them at a )rice still higher, the gain taxable to the donee is the difference between the price realized by him and the price paid by the donor. P. 481. TAFT v. …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"22, 35","1":"33, 37, 42, 44, 45","2":"10"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Phellis","cite":"257 U.S. 156","y":1921,"id":"scdb/1921-022","sc":1,"op":2,"ar":2,"sum":"1. Substance and not form should control in the application of the Sixteenth Amendment and the income tax laws enacted under it.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"20","1":"35, 36"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Safety Car Heating & Lighting Co.","cite":"297 U.S. 88","y":1936,"id":"scdb/1935-067","sc":1,"op":4,"ar":2,"sum":"A patent-owner began suit in 1912 to restrain infringements and for damages and profits. The litigation was pending on February 25, 1913, the effective date of the Sixteenth Amendment, and March 1, 1913, the effective date of the first statute enacted under it, and was continued for many years thereafter during which the patentowner obtained a decree finally sustaining the patent followed by a decree on accounting, …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"23, 35","2":"21"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Schillinger","cite":"27 F. Cas. 973","y":1876,"court":"C.C.S.D.N.Y.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.%20Cas./27/973/","by":{"0":"27","1":"20"}},{"t":"case","label":"Weiss v. Stearn","cite":"265 U.S. 242","y":1924,"id":"scdb/1923-154","sc":1,"sum":"Pursuant to an agreement, the stockholders of an Ohio corporation deposited 'with a trustee their certificates for all its capital stock ($5,000,000), and other parties deposited $7,500,000; the depositors organized a new Ohio corporation with authorized capital stock of $25,000,000, and powers like those of the old corporation; the new corporation took over the property, assets and business of the old one, assuming …","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"21, 55","1":"37"}},{"t":"case","label":"Whitlock’s Estate v. Comm’r","cite":"59 T.C. 490","y":1972,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/T.C./59/490/","op":1,"sum":"Finding that corporations’ “earnings and profits were as much petitioners’ income as if petitioners had received such earnings and profits themselves”.","sumSrc":"brief:0","by":{"0":"49"}},{"t":"const","label":"Amendment XVI","clauses":[["sixteenth-amendment","Sixteenth Amendment (Income Tax)"]],"text":"The Congress shall have power to lay and collect taxes on incomes, from whatever source derived, without apportionment among the several States, and without regard to any census or enumeration.","by":{"0":"4, 14, 17, 32, 34","2":"1, 3, 5, 6, 10, 16, 17, 21, 23, 24, 26"}},{"t":"const","label":"Article I, Section 2, Clause 3","clauses":[["apportionment","Apportionment and the Three-Fifths Clause"]],"text":"Representatives and direct Taxes shall be apportioned among the several States which may be included within this Union, according to their respective Numbers, which shall be determined by adding to the whole Number of free Persons, including those bound to Service for a Term of Years, and excluding Indians not taxed, three fifths of all other Persons. The actual Enumeration shall be made within three Years after the first Meeting of the Congress of the United States, and within every subsequent Term of ten Years, in such Manner as they shall by Law direct. The Number of Representatives shall not exceed one for every thirty Thousand, but each State shall have at Least one Representative; and until such enumeration shall be made, the State of New Hampshire shall be entitled to chuse three, Massachusetts eight, Rhode-Island and Providence Plantations one, Connecticut five, New-York six, New Jersey four, Pennsylvania eight, Delaware one, Maryland six, Virginia ten, North Carolina five, South Carolina five, and Georgia three.","by":{"0":"4, 12, 34"}},{"t":"const","label":"Article I, Section 9, Clause 4","text":"No Capitation, or other direct, Tax shall be laid, unless in Proportion to the Census or enumeration herein before directed to be taken.","by":{"0":"4, 12, 34, 36"}},{"t":"reg","label":"26 C.F.R. § 1.965-1","url":"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-26/section-1.965-1","by":{"0":"9"}},{"t":"usc","label":"26 U.S.C. § 61","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/61","subs":["(a)"],"stext":{"head":"§61. Gross income defined","paras":[[0,"(a) General definition"],[0,"Except as otherwise provided in this subtitle, gross income means all income from whatever source derived, including (but not limited to) the following items:"],[1,"(1) Compensation for services, including fees, commissions, fringe benefits, and similar items;"],[1,"(2) Gross income derived from business;"],[1,"(3) Gains derived from dealings in property;"],[1,"(4) Interest;"],[1,"(5) Rents;"],[1,"(6) Royalties;"],[1,"(7) Dividends;"],[1,"(8) Annuities;"],[1,"(9) Income from life insurance and endowment contracts;"],[1,"(10) Pensions;"],[1,"(11) Income from discharge of indebtedness;"],[1,"(12) Distributive share of partnership gross income;"],[1,"(13) Income in respect of a decedent; and"],[1,"(14) Income from an interest in an estate or trust."],[0,"(b) Cross references"],[2,"For items specifically included in gross income, see part II (sec. 71 and following). For items specifically excluded from gross income, see part III (sec. 101 and following)."]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/26/61?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"head":"§61. Gross income defined","tname":"Internal Revenue Code","by":{"0":"11, 37","1":"12, 39"}},{"t":"usc","label":"26 U.S.C. § 245A","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/245A","subs":["(a)","(c)(1)(A)"],"stext":{"head":"§245A. Deduction for foreign source-portion of dividends received by domestic corporations from specified 10-percent owned foreign corporations","paras":[[0,"(a) In general"],[0,"In the case of any dividend received from a specified 10-percent owned foreign corporation by a domestic corporation which is a United States shareholder with respect to such foreign corporation, there shall be allowed as a deduction an amount equal to the foreign-source portion of such dividend."],[0,"(b) Specified 10-percent owned foreign corporation"],[0,"For purposes of this section—"],[0,"(1) In general"],[1,"The term \"specified 10-percent owned foreign corporation\" means any foreign corporation with respect to which any domestic corporation is a United States shareholder with respect to such corporation."],[0,"(2) Exclusion of passive foreign investment companies"],[1,"Such term shall not include any corporation which is a passive foreign investment company (as defined in section 1297) with respect to the shareholder and which is not a controlled foreign corporation."],[0,"(c) Foreign-source portion"],[0,"For purposes of this section—"],[0,"(1) In general"],[1,"The foreign-source portion of any dividend from a specified 10-percent owned foreign corporation is an amount which bears the same ratio to such dividend as—"],[2,"(A) the undistributed foreign earnings of the specified 10-percent owned foreign corporation, bears to"],[2,"(B) the total undistributed earnings of such foreign corporation."],[0,"(2) Undistributed earnings"],[1,"The term \"undistributed earnings\" means the amount of the earnings and profits of the specified 10-percent owned foreign corporation (computed in accordance with sections 964(a) and 986)—"],[2,"(A) as of the close of the taxable year of the specified 10-percent owned foreign corporation in which the dividend is distributed, and"],[2,"(B) without diminution by reason of dividends distributed during such taxable year."],[0,"(3) Undistributed foreign earnings"],[1,"The term \"undistributed foreign earnings\" means the portion of the undistributed earnings which is attributable to neither—"],[2,"(A) income described in subparagraph (A) of section 245(a)(5), nor"],[2,"(B) dividends described in subparagraph (B) of such section (determined without regard to section 245(a)(12))."],[0,"(d) Disallowance of foreign tax credit, etc."],[0,"(1) In general"],[1,"No credit shall be allowed under section 901 for any taxes paid or accrued (or treated as paid or accrued) with respect to any dividend for which a deduction is allowed under this section."],[0,"(2) Denial of deduction"],[1,"No deduction shall be allowed under this chapter for any tax for which credit is not allowable under section 901 by reason of paragraph (1) (determined by treating the taxpayer as having elected the benefits of subpart A of part III of subchapter N)."],[0,"(e) Special rules for hybrid dividends"],[0,"(1) In general"],[1,"Subsection (a) shall not apply to any dividend received by a United States shareholder from a controlled foreign corporation if the dividend is a hybrid dividend."],[0,"(2) Hybrid dividends of tiered corporations"],[1,"If a controlled foreign corporation with respect to which a domestic corporation is a United States shareholder receives a hybrid dividend from any other controlled foreign corporation with respect to which such domestic corporation is also a United States shareholder, then, notwithstanding any other provision of this title—"],[2,"(A) the hybrid dividend shall be treated for purposes of section 951(a)(1)(A) as subpart F income of the receiving controlled foreign corporation for the taxable year of the controlled foreign corporation in which the dividend was received, and"],[2,"(B) the United States shareholder shall include in gross income an amount equal to the shareholder's pro rata share (determined in the same manner as under section 951(a)(2)) of the subpart F income described in subparagraph (A)."],[0,"(3) Denial of foreign tax credit, etc."],[1,"The rules of subsection (d) shall apply to any hybrid dividend received by, or any amount included under paragraph (2) in the gross income of, a United States shareholder."],[0,"(4) Hybrid dividend"],[1,"The term \"hybrid dividend\" means an amount received from a controlled foreign corporation—"],[2,"(A) for which a deduction would be allowed under subsection (a) but for this subsection, and"],[2,"(B) for which the controlled foreign corporation received a deduction (or other tax benefit) with respect to any income, war profits, or excess profits taxes imposed by any foreign country or possession of the United States."],[0,"(f) Special rule for purging distributions of passive foreign investment companies"],[0,"Any amount which is treated as a dividend under section 1291(d)(2)(B) shall not be treated as a dividend for purposes of this section."],[0,"(g) Regulations"],[0,"The Secretary shall prescribe such regulations or other guidance as may be necessary or appropriate to carry out the provisions of this section, including regulations for the treatment of United States shareholders owning stock of a specified 10 percent 1 owned foreign corporation through a partnership."]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/26/245A?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"head":"§245A. Deduction for foreign source-portion of dividends received by domestic corporations from specified 10-percent owned foreign corporations","tname":"Internal Revenue Code","by":{"0":"10","1":"5, 6, 5, 5"}},{"t":"usc","label":"26 U.S.C. § 305","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/305","subs":["(c)"],"stext":{"head":"§305. Distributions of stock and stock rights","paras":[[0,"(a) General rule"],[0,"Except as otherwise provided in this section, gross income does not include the amount of any distribution of the stock of a corporation made by such corporation to its shareholders with respect to its stock."],[0,"(b) Exceptions"],[0,"Subsection (a) shall not apply to a distribution by a corporation of its stock, and the distribution shall be treated as a distribution of property to which section 301 applies—"],[0,"(1) Distributions in lieu of money"],[1,"If the distribution is, at the election of any of the shareholders (whether exercised before or after the declaration thereof), payable either—"],[2,"(A) in its stock, or"],[2,"(B) in property."],[0,"(2) Disproportionate distributions"],[1,"If the distribution (or a series of distributions of which such distribution is one) has the result of—"],[2,"(A) the receipt of property by some shareholders, and"],[2,"(B) an increase in the proportionate interests of other shareholders in the assets or earnings and profits of the corporation."],[0,"(3) Distributions of common and preferred stock"],[1,"If the distribution (or a series of distributions of which such distribution is one) has the result of—"],[2,"(A) the receipt of preferred stock by some common shareholders, and"],[2,"(B) the receipt of common stock by other common shareholders."],[0,"(4) Distributions on preferred stock"],[1,"If the distribution is with respect to preferred stock, other than an increase in the conversion ratio of convertible preferred stock made solely to take account of a stock dividend or stock split with respect to the stock into which such convertible stock is convertible."],[0,"(5) Distributions of convertible preferred stock"],[1,"If the distribution is of convertible preferred stock, unless it is established to the satisfaction of the Secretary that such distribution will not have the result described in paragraph (2)."],[0,"(c) Certain transactions treated as distributions"],[0,"For purposes of this section and section 301, the Secretary shall prescribe regulations under which a change in conversion ratio, a change in redemption price, a difference between redemption price and issue price, a redemption which is treated as a distribution to which section 301 applies, or any transaction (including a recapitalization) having a similar effect on the interest of any shareholder shall be treated as a distribution with respect to any shareholder whose proportionate interest in the earnings and profits or assets of the corporation is increased by such change, difference, redemption, or similar transaction. Regulations prescribed under the preceding sentence shall provide that—"],[1,"(1) where the issuer of stock is required to redeem the stock at a specified time or the holder of stock has the option to require the issuer to redeem the stock, a redemption premium resulting from such requirement or option shall be treated as reasonable only if the amount of such premium does not exceed the amount determined under the principles of section 1273(a)(3),"],[1,"(2) a redemption premium shall not fail to be treated as a distribution (or series of distributions) merely because the stock is callable, and"],[1,"(3) in any case in which a redemption premium is treated as a distribution (or series of distributions), such premium shall be taken into account under principles similar to the principles of section 1272(a)."],[0,"(d) Definitions"],[0,"(1) Rights to acquire stock"],[1,"For purposes of this section, the term \"stock\" includes rights to acquire such stock."],[0,"(2) Shareholders"],[1,"For purposes of subsections (b) and (c), the term \"shareholder\" includes a holder of rights or of convertible securities."],[0,"(e) Treatment of purchaser of stripped preferred stock"],[0,"(1) In general"],[1,"If any person purchases after April 30, 1993, any stripped preferred stock, then such person, while holding such stock, shall include in gross income amounts equal to the amounts which would have been so includible if such stripped preferred stock were a bond issued on the purchase date and having original issue discount equal to the excess, if any, of—"],[2,"(A) the redemption price for such stock, over"],[2,"(B) the price at which such person purchased such stock."],[1,"The preceding sentence shall also apply in the case of any person whose basis in such stock is determined by reference to the basis in the hands of such purchaser."],[0,"(2) Basis adjustments"],[1,"Appropriate adjustments to basis shall be made for amounts includible in gross income under paragraph (1)."],[0,"(3) Tax treatment of person stripping stock"],[1,"If any person strips the rights to 1 or more dividends from any stock described in paragraph (5)(B) and after April 30, 1993, disposes of such dividend rights, for purposes of paragraph (1), such person shall be treated as having purchased the stripped preferred stock on the date of such disposition for a purchase price equal to such person's adjusted basis in such stripped preferred stock."],[0,"(4) Amounts treated as ordinary income"],[1,"Any amount included in gross income under paragraph (1) shall be treated as ordinary income."],[0,"(5) Stripped preferred stock"],[1,"For purposes of this subsection—"],[0,"(A) In general"],[2,"The term \"stripped preferred stock\" means any stock described in subparagraph (B) if there has been a separation in ownership between such stock and any dividend on such stock which has not become payable."],[0,"(B) Description of stock"],[2,"Stock is described in this subsection if such stock—"],[3,"(i) is limited and preferred as to dividends and does not participate in corporate growth to any significant extent, and"],[3,"(ii) has a fixed redemption price."],[0,"(6) Purchase"],[1,"For purposes of this subsection, the term \"purchase\" means—"],[2,"(A) any acquisition of stock, where"],[2,"(B) the basis of such stock is not determined in whole or in part by the reference to the adjusted basis of such stock in the hands of the person from whom acquired."],[0,"(7) Cross reference"],[2,"For treatment of stripped interests in certain accounts or entities holding preferred stock, see section 1286(e)."],[0,"(f) Cross references"],[1,"For special rules—"],[2,"(1) Relating to the receipt of stock and stock rights in corporate organizations and reorganizations, see part III (sec. 351 and following)."],[2,"(2) In the case of a distribution which results in a gift, see section 2501 and following."],[2,"(3) In the case of a distribution which has the effect of the payment of compensation, see section 61(a)(1)."]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/26/305?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"op":1,"head":"§305. Distributions of stock and stock rights","tname":"Internal Revenue Code","by":{"0":"39","1":"31"}},{"t":"usc","label":"26 U.S.C. § 475","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/475","subs":["(a)"],"stext":{"head":"§475. Mark to market accounting method for dealers in securities","paras":[[0,"(a) General rule"],[0,"Notwithstanding any other provision of this subpart, the following rules shall apply to securities held by a dealer in securities:"],[1,"(1) Any security which is inventory in the hands of the dealer shall be included in inventory at its fair market value."],[1,"(2) In the case of any security which is not inventory in the hands of the dealer and which is held at the close of any taxable year—"],[2,"(A) the dealer shall recognize gain or loss as if such security were sold for its fair market value on the last business day of such taxable year, and"],[2,"(B) any gain or loss shall be taken into account for such taxable year."],[1,"Proper adjustment shall be made in the amount of any gain or loss subsequently realized for gain or loss taken into account under the preceding sentence. The Secretary may provide by regulations for the application of this paragraph at times other than the times provided in this paragraph."],[0,"(b) Exceptions"],[0,"(1) In general"],[1,"Subsection (a) shall not apply to—"],[2,"(A) any security held for investment,"],[2,"(B)(i) any security described in subsection (c)(2)(C) which is acquired (including originated) by the taxpayer in the ordinary course of a trade or business of the taxpayer and which is not held for sale, and (ii) any obligation to acquire a security described in clause (i) if such obligation is entered into in the ordinary course of such trade or business and is not held for sale, and"],[2,"(C) any security which is a hedge with respect to—"],[3,"(i) a security to which subsection (a) does not apply, or"],[3,"(ii) a position, right to income, or a liability which is not a security in the hands of the taxpayer."],[1,"To the extent provided in regulations, subparagraph (C) shall not apply to any security held by a person in its capacity as a dealer in securities."],[0,"(2) Identification required"],[1,"A security shall not be treated as described in subparagraph (A), (B), or (C) of paragraph (1), as the case may be, unless such security is clearly identified in the dealer's records as being described in such subparagraph before the close of the day on which it was acquired, originated, or entered into (or such other time as the Secretary may by regulations prescribe)."],[0,"(3) Securities subsequently not exempt"],[1,"If a security ceases to be described in paragraph (1) at any time after it was identified as such under paragraph (2), subsection (a) shall apply to any changes in value of the security occurring after the cessation."],[0,"(4) Special rule for property held for investment"],[1,"To the extent provided in regulations, subparagraph (A) of paragraph (1) shall not apply to any security described in subparagraph (D) or (E) of subsection (c)(2) which is held by a dealer in such securities."],[0,"(c) Definitions"],[0,"For purposes of this section—"],[0,"(1) Dealer in securities defined"],[1,"The term \"dealer in securities\" means a taxpayer who—"],[2,"(A) regularly purchases securities from or sells securities to customers in the ordinary course of a trade or business; or"],[2,"(B) regularly offers to enter into, assume, offset, assign or otherwise terminate positions in securities with customers in the ordinary course of a trade or business."],[0,"(2) Security defined"],[1,"The term \"security\" means any—"],[2,"(A) share of stock in a corporation;"],[2,"(B) partnership or beneficial ownership interest in a widely held or publicly traded partnership or trust;"],[2,"(C) note, bond, debenture, or other evidence of indebtedness;"],[2,"(D) interest rate, currency, or equity notional principal contract;"],[2,"(E) evidence of an interest in, or a derivative financial instrument in, any security described in subparagraph (A), (B), (C), or (D), or any currency, including any option, forward contract, short position, and any similar financial instrument in such a security or currency; and"],[2,"(F) position which—"],[3,"(i) is not a security described in subparagraph (A), (B), (C), (D), or (E),"],[3,"(ii) is a hedge with respect to such a security, and"],[3,"(iii) is clearly identified in the dealer's records as being described in this subparagraph before the close of the day on which it was acquired or entered into (or such other time as the Secretary may by regulations prescribe)."],[1,"Subparagraph (E) shall not include any contract to which section 1256(a) applies."],[0,"(3) Hedge"],[1,"The term \"hedge\" means any position which manages the dealer's risk of interest rate or price changes or currency fluctuations, including any position which is reasonably expected to become a hedge within 60 days after the acquisition of the position."],[0,"(4) Special rules for certain receivables"],[0,"(A) In general"],[2,"Paragraph (2)(C) shall not include any nonfinancial customer paper."],[0,"(B) Nonfinancial customer paper"],[2,"For purposes of subparagraph (A), the term \"nonfinancial customer paper\" means any receivable which—"],[3,"(i) is a note, bond, debenture, or other evidence of indebtedness;"],[3,"(ii) arises out of the sale of nonfinancial goods or services by a person the principal activity of which is the selling or providing of nonfinancial goods or services; and"],[3,"(iii) is held by such person (or a person who bears a relationship to such person described in section 267(b) or 707(b)) at all times since issue."],[0,"(d) Special rules"],[0,"For purposes of this section—"],[0,"(1) Coordination with certain rules"],[1,"The rules of sections 263(g), 263A, and 1256(a) shall not apply to securities to which subsection (a) applies, and section 1091 shall not apply (and section 1092 shall apply) to any loss recognized under subsection (a)."],[0,"(2) Improper identification"],[1,"If a taxpayer—"],[2,"(A) identifies any security under subsection (b)(2) as being described in subsection (b)(1) and such security is not so described, or"],[2,"(B) fails under subsection (c)(2)(F)(iii) to identify any position which is described in subsection (c)(2)(F) (without regard to clause (iii) thereof) at the time such identification is required,"],[1,"the provisions of subsection (a) shall apply to such security or position, except that any loss under this section prior to the disposition of the security or position shall be recognized only to the extent of gain previously recognized under this section (and not previously taken into account under this paragraph) with respect to such security or position."],[0,"(3) Character of gain or loss"],[0,"(A) In general"],[2,"Except as provided in subparagraph (B) or section 1236(b)—"],[0,"(i) In general"],[3,"Any gain or loss with respect to a security under subsection (a)(2) shall be treated as ordinary income or loss."],[0,"(ii) Special rule for dispositions"],[3,"If—"],[4,"(I) gain or loss is recognized with respect to a security before the close of the taxable year, and"],[4,"(II) subsection (a)(2) would have applied if the security were held as of the close of the taxable year,"],[0,"such gain or loss shall be treated as ordinary income or loss."],[0,"(B) Exception"],[2,"Subparagraph (A) shall not apply to any gain or loss which is allocable to a period during which—"],[3,"(i) the security is described in subsection (b)(1)(C) (without regard to subsection (b)(2)),"],[3,"(ii) the security is held by a person other than in connection with its activities as a dealer in securities, or"],[3,"(iii) the security is improperly identified (within the meaning of subparagraph (A) or (B) of paragraph (2))."],[0,"(e) Election of mark to market for dealers in commodities"],[0,"(1) In general"],[1,"In the case of a dealer in commodities who elects the application of this subsection, this section shall apply to commodities held by such dealer in the same manner as this section applies to securities held by a dealer in securities."],[0,"(2) Commodity"],[1,"For purposes of this subsection and subsection (f), the term \"commodity\" means—"],[2,"(A) any commodity which is actively traded (within the meaning of section 1092(d)(1));"],[2,"(B) any notional principal contract with respect to any commodity described in subparagraph (A);"],[2,"(C) any evidence of an interest in, or a derivative instrument in, any commodity described in subparagraph (A) or (B), including any option, forward contract, futures contract, short position, and any similar instrument in such a commodity; and"],[2,"(D) any position which—"],[3,"(i) is not a commodity described in subparagraph (A), (B), or (C),"],[3,"(ii) is a hedge with respect to such a commodity, and"],[3,"(iii) is clearly identified in the taxpayer's records as being described in this subparagraph before the close of the day on which it was acquired or entered into (or such other time as the Secretary may by regulations prescribe)."],[0,"(3) Election"],[1,"An election under this subsection may be made without the consent of the Secretary. Such an election, once made, shall apply to the taxable year for which made and all subsequent taxable years unless revoked with the consent of the Secretary."],[0,"(f) Election of mark to market for traders in securities or commodities"],[0,"(1) Traders in securities"],[0,"(A) In general"],[2,"In the case of a person who is engaged in a trade or business as a trader in securities and who elects to have this paragraph apply to such trade or business—"],[3,"(i) such person shall recognize gain or loss on any security held in connection with such trade or business at the close of any taxable year as if such security were sold for its fair market value on the last business day of such taxable year, and"],[3,"(ii) any gain or loss shall be taken into account for such taxable year."]],"more":true,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/26/475?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"head":"§475. Mark to market accounting method for dealers in securities","tname":"Internal Revenue Code","by":{"0":"52","1":"31"}},{"t":"usc","label":"26 U.S.C. § 531","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/531","by":{"0":"39"}},{"t":"usc","label":"26 U.S.C. § 701","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/701","stext":{"head":"§701. Partners, not partnership, subject to tax","paras":[[0,"A partnership as such shall not be subject to the income tax imposed by this chapter. Persons carrying on business as partners shall be liable for income tax only in their separate or individual capacities."]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/26/701?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"op":1,"head":"§701. Partners, not partnership, subject to tax","tname":"Internal Revenue Code","by":{"0":"51"}},{"t":"usc","label":"26 U.S.C. § 702","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/702","subs":["(a)","(b)"],"stext":{"head":"§702. Income and credits of partner","paras":[[0,"(a) General rule"],[0,"In determining his income tax, each partner shall take into account separately his distributive share of the partnership's—"],[1,"(1) gains and losses from sales or exchanges of capital assets held for not more than 1 year,"],[1,"(2) gains and losses from sales or exchanges of capital assets held for more than 1 year,"],[1,"(3) gains and losses from sales or exchanges of property described in section 1231 (relating to certain property used in a trade or business and involuntary conversions),"],[1,"(4) charitable contributions (as defined in section 170(c)),"],[1,"(5) dividends with respect to which section 1(h)(11) or part VIII of subchapter B applies,"],[1,"(6) taxes, described in section 901, paid or accrued to foreign countries and to possessions of the United States,"],[1,"(7) other items of income, gain, loss, deduction, or credit, to the extent provided by regulations prescribed by the Secretary, and"],[1,"(8) taxable income or loss, exclusive of items requiring separate computation under other paragraphs of this subsection."],[0,"(b) Character of items constituting distributive share"],[0,"The character of any item of income, gain, loss, deduction, or credit included in a partner's distributive share under paragraphs (1) through (7) of subsection (a) shall be determined as if such item were realized directly from the source from which realized by the partnership, or incurred in the same manner as incurred by the partnership."],[0,"(c) Gross income of a partner"],[0,"In any case where it is necessary to determine the gross income of a partner for purposes of this title, such amount shall include his distributive share of the gross income of the partnership."],[0,"(d) Cross reference"],[2,"For rules relating to procedures for determining the tax treatment of partnership items see subchapter C of chapter 63 (section 6221 and following)."]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/26/702?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"head":"§702. Income and credits of partner","tname":"Internal Revenue Code","by":{"0":"51","1":"29, 29"}},{"t":"usc","label":"26 U.S.C. § 817A","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/817A","subs":["(b)"],"by":{"0":"52, 53","1":"31"}},{"t":"usc","label":"26 U.S.C. § 877A","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/877A","subs":["(a)","(a)(1)","(b)(1)","(b)(4)(A)"],"stext":{"head":"§877A. Tax responsibilities of expatriation","paras":[[0,"(a) General rules"],[0,"For purposes of this subtitle—"],[0,"(1) Mark to market"],[1,"All property of a covered expatriate shall be treated as sold on the day before the expatriation date for its fair market value."],[0,"(2) Recognition of gain or loss"],[1,"In the case of any sale under paragraph (1)—"],[2,"(A) notwithstanding any other provision of this title, any gain arising from such sale shall be taken into account for the taxable year of the sale, and"],[2,"(B) any loss arising from such sale shall be taken into account for the taxable year of the sale to the extent otherwise provided by this title, except that section 1091 shall not apply to any such loss."],[1,"Proper adjustment shall be made in the amount of any gain or loss subsequently realized for gain or loss taken into account under the preceding sentence, determined without regard to paragraph (3)."],[0,"(3) Exclusion for certain gain"],[0,"(A) In general"],[2,"The amount which would (but for this paragraph) be includible in the gross income of any individual by reason of paragraph (1) shall be reduced (but not below zero) by $600,000."],[0,"(B) Adjustment for inflation"],[0,"(i) In general"],[3,"In the case of any taxable year beginning in a calendar year after 2008, the dollar amount in subparagraph (A) shall be increased by an amount equal to—"],[4,"(I) such dollar amount, multiplied by"],[4,"(II) the cost-of-living adjustment determined under section 1(f)(3) for the calendar year in which the taxable year begins, by substituting \"calendar year 2007\" for \"calendar year 2016\" in subparagraph (A)(ii) thereof."],[0,"(ii) Rounding"],[3,"If any amount as adjusted under clause (i) is not a multiple of $1,000, such amount shall be rounded to the nearest multiple of $1,000."],[0,"(b) Election to defer tax"],[0,"(1) In general"],[1,"If the taxpayer elects the application of this subsection with respect to any property treated as sold by reason of subsection (a), the time for payment of the additional tax attributable to such property shall be extended until the due date of the return for the taxable year in which such property is disposed of (or, in the case of property disposed of in a transaction in which gain is not recognized in whole or in part, until such other date as the Secretary may prescribe)."],[0,"(2) Determination of tax with respect to property"],[1,"For purposes of paragraph (1), the additional tax attributable to any property is an amount which bears the same ratio to the additional tax imposed by this chapter for the taxable year solely by reason of subsection (a) as the gain taken into account under subsection (a) with respect to such property bears to the total gain taken into account under subsection (a) with respect to all property to which subsection (a) applies."],[0,"(3) Termination of extension"],[1,"The due date for payment of tax may not be extended under this subsection later than the due date for the return of tax imposed by this chapter for the taxable year which includes the date of death of the expatriate (or, if earlier, the time that the security provided with respect to the property fails to meet the requirements of paragraph (4), unless the taxpayer corrects such failure within the time specified by the Secretary)."],[0,"(4) Security"],[0,"(A) In general"],[2,"No election may be made under paragraph (1) with respect to any property unless adequate security is provided with respect to such property."],[0,"(B) Adequate security"],[2,"For purposes of subparagraph (A), security with respect to any property shall be treated as adequate security if—"],[3,"(i) it is a bond which is furnished to, and accepted by, the Secretary, which is conditioned on the payment of tax (and interest thereon), and which meets the requirements of section 6325, or"],[3,"(ii) it is another form of security for such payment (including letters of credit) that meets such requirements as the Secretary may prescribe."],[0,"(5) Waiver of certain rights"],[1,"No election may be made under paragraph (1) unless the taxpayer makes an irrevocable waiver of any right under any treaty of the United States which would preclude assessment or collection of any tax imposed by reason of this section."],[0,"(6) Elections"],[1,"An election under paragraph (1) shall only apply to property described in the election and, once made, is irrevocable."],[0,"(7) Interest"],[1,"For purposes of section 6601, the last date for the payment of tax shall be determined without regard to the election under this subsection."],[0,"(c) Exception for certain property"],[0,"Subsection (a) shall not apply to—"],[1,"(1) any deferred compensation item (as defined in subsection (d)(4)),"],[1,"(2) any specified tax deferred account (as defined in subsection (e)(2)), and"],[1,"(3) any interest in a nongrantor trust (as defined in subsection (f)(3))."],[0,"(d) Treatment of deferred compensation items"],[0,"(1) Withholding on eligible deferred compensation items"],[0,"(A) In general"],[2,"In the case of any eligible deferred compensation item, the payor shall deduct and withhold from any taxable payment to a covered expatriate with respect to such item a tax equal to 30 percent thereof."],[0,"(B) Taxable payment"],[2,"For purposes of subparagraph (A), the term \"taxable payment\" means with respect to a covered expatriate any payment to the extent it would be includible in the gross income of the covered expatriate if such expatriate continued to be subject to tax as a citizen or resident of the United States. A deferred compensation item shall be taken into account as a payment under the preceding sentence when such item would be so includible."],[0,"(2) Other deferred compensation items"],[1,"In the case of any deferred compensation item which is not an eligible deferred compensation item—"],[2,"(A)(i) with respect to any deferred compensation item to which clause (ii) does not apply, an amount equal to the present value of the covered expatriate's accrued benefit shall be treated as having been received by such individual on the day before the expatriation date as a distribution under the plan, and"],[2,"(ii) with respect to any deferred compensation item referred to in paragraph (4)(D), the rights of the covered expatriate to such item shall be treated as becoming transferable and not subject to a substantial risk of forfeiture on the day before the expatriation date,"],[2,"(B) no early distribution tax shall apply by reason of such treatment, and"],[2,"(C) appropriate adjustments shall be made to subsequent distributions from the plan to reflect such treatment."],[0,"(3) Eligible deferred compensation items"],[1,"For purposes of this subsection, the term \"eligible deferred compensation item\" means any deferred compensation item with respect to which—"],[2,"(A) the payor of such item is—"],[3,"(i) a United States person, or"],[3,"(ii) a person who is not a United States person but who elects to be treated as a United States person for purposes of paragraph (1) and meets such requirements as the Secretary may provide to ensure that the payor will meet the requirements of paragraph (1), and"],[2,"(B) the covered expatriate—"],[3,"(i) notifies the payor of his status as a covered expatriate, and"],[3,"(ii) makes an irrevocable waiver of any right to claim any reduction under any treaty with the United States in withholding on such item."],[0,"(4) Deferred compensation item"],[1,"For purposes of this subsection, the term \"deferred compensation item\" means—"],[2,"(A) any interest in a plan or arrangement described in section 219(g)(5),"],[2,"(B) any interest in a foreign pension plan or similar retirement arrangement or program,"],[2,"(C) any item of deferred compensation, and"],[2,"(D) any property, or right to property, which the individual is entitled to receive in connection with the performance of services to the extent not previously taken into account under section 83 or in accordance with section 83."],[0,"(5) Exception"],[1,"Paragraphs (1) and (2) shall not apply to any deferred compensation item to the extent attributable to services performed outside the United States while the covered expatriate was not a citizen or resident of the United States."],[0,"(6) Special rules"],[0,"(A) Application of withholding rules"],[2,"Rules similar to the rules of subchapter B of chapter 3 shall apply for purposes of this subsection."],[0,"(B) Application of tax"],[2,"Any item subject to the withholding tax imposed under paragraph (1) shall be subject to tax under section 871."],[0,"(C) Coordination with other withholding requirements"],[2,"Any item subject to withholding under paragraph (1) shall not be subject to withholding under section 1441 or chapter 24."],[0,"(e) Treatment of specified tax deferred accounts"],[0,"(1) Account treated as distributed"],[1,"In the case of any interest in a specified tax deferred account held by a covered expatriate on the day before the expatriation date—"],[2,"(A) the covered expatriate shall be treated as receiving a distribution of his entire interest in such account on the day before the expatriation date,"],[2,"(B) no early distribution tax shall apply by reason of such treatment, and"],[2,"(C) appropriate adjustments shall be made to subsequent distributions from the account to reflect such treatment."],[0,"(2) Specified tax deferred account"],[1,"For purposes of paragraph (1), the term \"specified tax deferred account\" means an individual retirement plan (as defined in section 7701(a)(37)) other than any arrangement described in subsection (k) or (p) of section 408, a qualified tuition program (as defined in section 529), a qualified ABLE program (as defined in section 529A), a Coverdell education savings account (as defined in section 530), a health savings account (as defined in section 223), and an Archer MSA (as defined in section 220)."]],"more":true,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/26/877A?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"head":"§877A. Tax responsibilities of expatriation","tname":"Internal Revenue Code","by":{"0":"52","1":"30, 31, 31, 31","2":"21"}},{"t":"usc","label":"26 U.S.C. § 951","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/951","subs":["(a)(1)","(a)(1)(A)","(b)"],"stext":{"head":"§951. Amounts included in gross income of United States shareholders","paras":[[0,"(a) Amounts included"],[0,"(1) In general"],[1,"If a foreign corporation is a controlled foreign corporation at any time during any taxable year, every person who is a United States shareholder (as defined in subsection (b)) of such corporation and who owns (within the meaning of section 958(a)) stock in such corporation on the last day, in such year, on which such corporation is a controlled foreign corporation shall include in his gross income, for his taxable year in which or with which such taxable year of the corporation ends—"],[2,"(A) his pro rata share (determined under paragraph (2)) of the corporation's subpart F income for such year, and"],[2,"(B) the amount determined under section 956 with respect to such shareholder for such year (but only to the extent not excluded from gross income under section 959(a)(2))."],[0,"(2) Pro rata share of subpart F income"],[1,"The pro rata share referred to in paragraph (1)(A)(i) in the case of any United States shareholder is the amount—"],[2,"(A) which would have been distributed with respect to the stock which such shareholder owns (within the meaning of section 958(a)) in such corporation if on the last day, in its taxable year, on which the corporation is a controlled foreign corporation it had distributed pro rata to its shareholders an amount (i) which bears the same ratio to its subpart F income for the taxable year, as (ii) the part of such year during which the corporation is a controlled foreign corporation bears to the entire year, reduced by"],[2,"(B) the amount of distributions received by any other person during such year as a dividend with respect to such stock, but only to the extent of the dividend which would have been received if the distribution by the corporation had been the amount (i) which bears the same ratio to the subpart F income of such corporation for the taxable year, as (ii) the part of such year during which such shareholder did not own (within the meaning of section 958(a)) such stock bears to the entire year."],[1,"For purposes of subparagraph (B), any gain included in the gross income of any person as a dividend under section 1248 shall be treated as a distribution received by such person with respect to the stock involved."],[0,"(b) United States shareholder defined"],[0,"For purposes of this title, the term \"United States shareholder\" means, with respect to any foreign corporation, a United States person (as defined in section 957(c)) who owns (within the meaning of section 958(a)), or is considered as owning by applying the rules of ownership of section 958(b), 10 percent or more of the total combined voting power of all classes of stock entitled to vote of such foreign corporation, or 10 percent or more of the total value of shares of all classes of stock of such foreign corporation."],[0,"(c) Coordination with passive foreign investment company provisions"],[0,"If, but for this subsection, an amount would be included in the gross income of a United States shareholder for any taxable year both under subsection (a)(1)(A)(i) and under section 1293 (relating to current taxation of income from certain passive foreign investment companies), such amount shall be included in the gross income of such shareholder only under subsection (a)(1)(A)."]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/26/951?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"op":5,"head":"§951. Amounts included in gross income of United States shareholders","tname":"Internal Revenue Code","by":{"0":"10","1":"4-7, 10, 26-29, 32, 40, 42-44, 46, 49, 28, 28, 28"}},{"t":"usc","label":"26 U.S.C. § 952","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/952","stext":{"head":"§952. Subpart F income defined","paras":[[0,"(a) In general"],[0,"For purposes of this subpart, the term \"subpart F income\" means, in the case of any controlled foreign corporation, the sum of—"],[1,"(1) insurance income (as defined under section 953),"],[1,"(2) the foreign base company income (as determined under section 954),"],[1,"(3) an amount equal to the product of—"],[2,"(A) the income of such corporation other than income which—"],[3,"(i) is attributable to earnings and profits of the foreign corporation included in the gross income of a United States person under section 951 (other than by reason of this paragraph), or"],[3,"(ii) is described in subsection (b),"],[2,"multiplied by"],[2,"(B) the international boycott factor (as determined under section 999),"],[1,"(4) the sum of the amounts of any illegal bribes, kickbacks, or other payments (within the meaning of section 162(c)) paid by or on behalf of the corporation during the taxable year of the corporation directly or indirectly to an official, employee, or agent in fact of a government, and"],[1,"(5) the income of such corporation derived from any foreign country during any period during which section 901(j) applies to such foreign country."],[0,"The payments referred to in paragraph (4) are payments which would be unlawful under the Foreign Corrupt Practices Act of 1977 if the payor were a United States person. For purposes of paragraph (5), the income described therein shall be reduced, under regulations prescribed by the Secretary, so as to take into account deductions (including taxes) properly allocable to such income."],[0,"(b) Exclusion of United States income"],[0,"In the case of a controlled foreign corporation, subpart F income does not include any item of income from sources within the United States which is effectively connected with the conduct by such corporation of a trade or business within the United States unless such item is exempt from taxation (or is subject to a reduced rate of tax) pursuant to a treaty obligation of the United States. For purposes of this subsection, any exemption (or reduction) with respect to the tax imposed by section 884 shall not be taken into account."],[0,"(c) Limitation"],[0,"(1) In general"],[0,"(A) Subpart F income limited to current earnings and profits"],[2,"For purposes of subsection (a), the subpart F income of any controlled foreign corporation for any taxable year shall not exceed the earnings and profits of such corporation for such taxable year."],[0,"(B) Certain prior year deficits may be taken into account"],[0,"(i) In general"],[3,"The amount included in the gross income of any United States shareholder under section 951(a)(1)(A) for any taxable year and attributable to a qualified activity shall be reduced by the amount of such shareholder's pro rata share of any qualified deficit."],[0,"(ii) Qualified deficit"],[3,"The term \"qualified deficit\" means any deficit in earnings and profits of the controlled foreign corporation for any prior taxable year which began after December 31, 1986, and for which the controlled foreign corporation was a controlled foreign corporation; but only to the extent such deficit—"],[4,"(I) is attributable to the same qualified activity as the activity giving rise to the income being offset, and"],[4,"(II) has not previously been taken into account under this subparagraph."],[0,"In determining the deficit attributable to qualified activities described in subclause (II) or (III) of clause (iii),1 deficits in earnings and profits (to the extent not previously taken into account under this section) for taxable years beginning after 1962 and before 1987 also shall be taken into account. In the case of the qualified activity described in clause (iii)(I),1 the rule of the preceding sentence shall apply, except that \"1982\" shall be substituted for \"1962\"."],[0,"(iii) Qualified activity"],[3,"For purposes of this paragraph, the term \"qualified activity\" means any activity giving rise to—"],[4,"(I) foreign base company sales income,"],[4,"(II) foreign base company services income,"],[4,"(III) in the case of a qualified insurance company, insurance income or foreign personal holding company income, or"],[4,"(IV) in the case of a qualified financial institution, foreign personal holding company income."],[0,"(iv) Pro rata share"],[3,"For purposes of this paragraph, the shareholder's pro rata share of any deficit for any prior taxable year shall be determined under rules similar to rules under section 951(a)(2) for whichever of the following yields the smaller share:"],[4,"(I) the close of the taxable year, or"],[4,"(II) the close of the taxable year in which the deficit arose."],[0,"(v) Qualified insurance company"],[3,"For purposes of this subparagraph, the term \"qualified insurance company\" means any controlled foreign corporation predominantly engaged in the active conduct of an insurance business in the taxable year and in the prior taxable years in which the deficit arose."],[0,"(vi) Qualified financial institution"],[3,"For purposes of this paragraph, the term \"qualified financial institution\" means any controlled foreign corporation predominantly engaged in the active conduct of a banking, financing, or similar business in the taxable year and in the prior taxable year in which the deficit arose."],[0,"(vii) Special rules for insurance income"],[0,"(I) In general"],[4,"An election may be made under this clause to have section 953(a) applied for purposes of this title without regard to the same country exception under paragraph (1)(A) thereof. Such election, once made, may be revoked only with the consent of the Secretary."],[0,"(II) Special rules for affiliated groups"],[4,"In the case of an affiliated group of corporations (within the meaning of section 1504 but without regard to section 1504(b)(3) and by substituting \"more than 50 percent\" for \"at least 80 percent\" each place it appears), no election may be made under subclause (I) for any controlled foreign corporation unless such election is made for all other controlled foreign corporations who are members of such group and who were created or organized under the laws of the same country as such controlled foreign corporation. For purposes of clause (v), in determining whether any controlled corporation described in the preceding sentence is a qualified insurance company, all such corporations shall be treated as 1 corporation."],[0,"(C) Certain deficits of member of the same chain of corporations may be taken into account"],[0,"(i) In general"],[3,"A controlled foreign corporation may elect to reduce the amount of its subpart F income for any taxable year which is attributable to any qualified activity by the amount of any deficit in earnings and profits of a qualified chain member for a taxable year ending with (or within) the taxable year of such controlled foreign corporation to the extent such deficit is attributable to such activity. To the extent any deficit reduces subpart F income under the preceding sentence, such deficit shall not be taken into account under subparagraph (B)."],[0,"(ii) Qualified chain member"],[3,"For purposes of this subparagraph, the term \"qualified chain member\" means, with respect to any controlled foreign corporation, any other corporation which is created or organized under the laws of the same foreign country as the controlled foreign corporation but only if—"],[4,"(I) all the stock of such other corporation (other than directors' qualifying shares) is owned at all times during the taxable year in which the deficit arose (directly or through 1 or more corporations other than the common parent) by such controlled foreign corporation, or"],[4,"(II) all the stock of such controlled foreign corporation (other than directors' qualifying shares) is owned at all times during the taxable year in which the deficit arose (directly or through 1 or more corporations other than the common parent) by such other corporation."],[0,"(iii) Coordination"],[3,"This subparagraph shall be applied after subparagraphs (A) and (B)."],[0,"(2) Recharacterization in subsequent taxable years"],[1,"If the subpart F income of any controlled foreign corporation for any taxable year was reduced by reason of paragraph (1)(A), any excess of the earnings and profits of such corporation for any subsequent taxable year over the subpart F income of such foreign corporation for such taxable year shall be recharacterized as subpart F income under rules similar to the rules applicable under section 904(f)(5)."],[0,"(3) Special rule for determining earnings and profits"],[1,"For purposes of this subsection, earnings and profits of any controlled foreign corporation shall be determined without regard to paragraphs (4), (5), and (6) of section 312(n). Under regulations, the preceding sentence shall not apply to the extent it would increase earnings and profits by an amount which was previously distributed by the controlled foreign corporation."],[0,"(d) Income derived from foreign country"],[0,"The Secretary shall prescribe such regulations as may be necessary or appropriate to carry out the purposes of subsection (a)(5), including regulations which treat income paid through 1 or more entities as derived from a foreign country to which section 901(j) applies if such income was, without regard to such entities, derived from such country."]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/26/952?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"head":"§952. Subpart F income defined","tname":"Internal Revenue Code","by":{"0":"45","1":"28"}},{"t":"usc","label":"26 U.S.C. § 953","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/953","by":{"0":"45"}},{"t":"usc","label":"26 U.S.C. § 954","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/954","by":{"0":"45"}},{"t":"usc","label":"26 U.S.C. § 957","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/957","subs":["(a)"],"stext":{"head":"§957. Controlled foreign corporations; United States persons","paras":[[0,"(a) General rule"],[0,"For purposes of this title, the term \"controlled foreign corporation\" means any foreign corporation if more than 50 percent of—"],[1,"(1) the total combined voting power of all classes of stock of such corporation entitled to vote, or"],[1,"(2) the total value of the stock of such corporation,"],[0,"is owned (within the meaning of section 958(a)), or is considered as owned by applying the rules of ownership of section 958(b), by United States shareholders on any day during the taxable year of such foreign corporation."],[0,"(b) Special rule for insurance"],[0,"For purposes only of taking into account income described in section 953(a) (relating to insurance income), the term \"controlled foreign corporation\" includes not only a foreign corporation as defined by subsection (a) but also one of which more than 25 percent of the total combined voting power of all classes of stock (or more than 25 percent of the total value of stock) is owned (within the meaning of section 958(a)), or is considered as owned by applying the rules of ownership of section 958(b), by United States shareholders on any day during the taxable year of such corporation, if the gross amount of premiums or other consideration in respect of the reinsurance or the issuing of insurance or annuity contracts not described in section 953(e)(2) exceeds 75 percent of the gross amount of all premiums or other consideration in respect of all risks."],[0,"(c) United States person"],[0,"For purposes of this subpart, the term \"United States person\" has the meaning assigned to it by section 7701(a)(30) except that—"],[1,"(1) with respect to a corporation organized under the laws of the Commonwealth of Puerto Rico, such term does not include an individual who is a bona fide resident of Puerto Rico, if a dividend received by such individual during the taxable year from such corporation would, for purposes of section 933(1), be treated as income derived from sources within Puerto Rico, and"],[1,"(2) with respect to a corporation organized under the laws of Guam, American Samoa, or the Northern Mariana Islands—"],[2,"(A) 80 percent or more of the gross income of which for the 3-year period ending at the close of the taxable year (or for such part of such period as such corporation or any predecessor has been in existence) was derived from sources within such a possession or was effectively connected with the conduct of a trade or business in such a possession, and"],[2,"(B) 50 percent or more of the gross income of which for such period (or part) was derived from the active conduct of a trade or business within such a possession,"],[1,"such term does not include an individual who is a bona fide resident of Guam, American Samoa, or the Northern Mariana Islands."],[0,"For purposes of subparagraphs (A) and (B) of paragraph (2), the determination as to whether income was derived from the active conduct of a trade or business within a possession shall be made under regulations prescribed by the Secretary."]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/26/957?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"head":"§957. Controlled foreign corporations; United States persons","tname":"Internal Revenue Code","by":{"0":"9","1":"39"}},{"t":"usc","label":"26 U.S.C. § 965","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/965","subs":["(a)","(a)(1)","(a)(2)","(c)","(e)(1)(B)","(h)"],"stext":{"head":"§965. Treatment of deferred foreign income upon transition to participation exemption system of taxation","paras":[[0,"(a) Treatment of deferred foreign income as subpart F income"],[0,"In the case of the last taxable year of a deferred foreign income corporation which begins before January 1, 2018, the subpart F income of such foreign corporation (as otherwise determined for such taxable year under section 952) shall be increased by the greater of—"],[1,"(1) the accumulated post-1986 deferred foreign income of such corporation determined as of November 2, 2017, or"],[1,"(2) the accumulated post-1986 deferred foreign income of such corporation determined as of December 31, 2017."],[0,"(b) Reduction in amounts included in gross income of United States shareholders of specified foreign corporations with deficits in earnings and profits"],[0,"(1) In general"],[1,"In the case of a taxpayer which is a United States shareholder with respect to at least one deferred foreign income corporation and at least one E&P deficit foreign corporation, the amount which would (but for this subsection) be taken into account under section 951(a)(1) by reason of subsection (a) as such United States shareholder's pro rata share of the subpart F income of each deferred foreign income corporation shall be reduced by the amount of such United States shareholder's aggregate foreign E&P deficit which is allocated under paragraph (2) to such deferred foreign income corporation."],[0,"(2) Allocation of aggregate foreign E&P deficit"],[1,"The aggregate foreign E&P deficit of any United States shareholder shall be allocated among the deferred foreign income corporations of such United States shareholder in an amount which bears the same proportion to such aggregate as—"],[2,"(A) such United States shareholder's pro rata share of the accumulated post-1986 deferred foreign income of each such deferred foreign income corporation, bears to"],[2,"(B) the aggregate of such United States shareholder's pro rata share of the accumulated post-1986 deferred foreign income of all deferred foreign income corporations of such United States shareholder."],[0,"(3) Definitions related to E&P deficits"],[1,"For purposes of this subsection—"],[0,"(A) Aggregate foreign E&P deficit"],[0,"(i) In general"],[3,"The term \"aggregate foreign E&P deficit\" means, with respect to any United States shareholder, the lesser of—"],[4,"(I) the aggregate of such shareholder's pro rata shares of the specified E&P deficits of the E&P deficit foreign corporations of such shareholder, or"],[4,"(II) the amount determined under paragraph (2)(B)."],[0,"(ii) Allocation of deficit"],[3,"If the amount described in clause (i)(II) is less than the amount described in clause (i)(I), then the shareholder shall designate, in such form and manner as the Secretary determines—"],[4,"(I) the amount of the specified E&P deficit which is to be taken into account for each E&P deficit corporation with respect to the taxpayer, and"],[4,"(II) in the case of an E&P deficit corporation which has a qualified deficit (as defined in section 952), the portion (if any) of the deficit taken into account under subclause (I) which is attributable to a qualified deficit, including the qualified activities to which such portion is attributable."],[0,"(B) E&P deficit foreign corporation"],[2,"The term \"E&P deficit foreign corporation\" means, with respect to any taxpayer, any specified foreign corporation with respect to which such taxpayer is a United States shareholder, if, as of November 2, 2017—"],[3,"(i) such specified foreign corporation has a deficit in post-1986 earnings and profits,"],[3,"(ii) such corporation was a specified foreign corporation, and"],[3,"(iii) such taxpayer was a United States shareholder of such corporation."],[0,"(C) Specified E&P deficit"],[2,"The term \"specified E&P deficit\" means, with respect to any E&P deficit foreign corporation, the amount of the deficit referred to in subparagraph (B)."],[0,"(4) Treatment of earnings and profits in future years"],[0,"(A) Reduced earnings and profits treated as previously taxed income when distributed"],[2,"For purposes of applying section 959 in any taxable year beginning with the taxable year described in subsection (a), with respect to any United States shareholder of a deferred foreign income corporation, an amount equal to such shareholder's reduction under paragraph (1) which is allocated to such deferred foreign income corporation under this subsection shall be treated as an amount which was included in the gross income of such United States shareholder under section 951(a)."],[0,"(B) E&P deficits"],[2,"For purposes of this title, with respect to any taxable year beginning with the taxable year described in subsection (a), a United States shareholder's pro rata share of the earnings and profits of any E&P deficit foreign corporation under this subsection shall be increased by the amount of the specified E&P deficit of such corporation taken into account by such shareholder under paragraph (1), and, for purposes of section 952, such increase shall be attributable to the same activity to which the deficit so taken into account was attributable."],[0,"(5) Netting among United States shareholders in same affiliated group"],[0,"(A) In general"],[2,"In the case of any affiliated group which includes at least one E&P net surplus shareholder and one E&P net deficit shareholder, the amount which would (but for this paragraph) be taken into account under section 951(a)(1) by reason of subsection (a) by each such E&P net surplus shareholder shall be reduced (but not below zero) by such shareholder's applicable share of the affiliated group's aggregate unused E&P deficit."],[0,"(B) E&P net surplus shareholder"],[2,"For purposes of this paragraph, the term \"E&P net surplus shareholder\" means any United States shareholder which would (determined without regard to this paragraph) take into account an amount greater than zero under section 951(a)(1) by reason of subsection (a)."],[0,"(C) E&P net deficit shareholder"],[2,"For purposes of this paragraph, the term \"E&P net deficit shareholder\" means any United States shareholder if—"],[3,"(i) the aggregate foreign E&P deficit with respect to such shareholder (as defined in paragraph (3)(A) without regard to clause (i)(II) thereof), exceeds"],[3,"(ii) the amount which would (but for this subsection) be taken into account by such shareholder under section 951(a)(1) by reason of subsection (a)."],[0,"(D) Aggregate unused E&P deficit"],[2,"For purposes of this paragraph—"],[0,"(i) In general"],[3,"The term \"aggregate unused E&P deficit\" means, with respect to any affiliated group, the lesser of—"],[4,"(I) the sum of the excesses described in subparagraph (C), determined with respect to each E&P net deficit shareholder in such group, or"],[4,"(II) the amount determined under subparagraph (E)(ii)."],[0,"(ii) Reduction with respect to E&P net deficit shareholders which are not wholly owned by the affiliated group"],[3,"If the group ownership percentage of any E&P net deficit shareholder is less than 100 percent, the amount of the excess described in subparagraph (C) which is taken into account under clause (i)(I) with respect to such E&P net deficit shareholder shall be such group ownership percentage of such amount."],[0,"(E) Applicable share"],[2,"For purposes of this paragraph, the term \"applicable share\" means, with respect to any E&P net surplus shareholder in any affiliated group, the amount which bears the same proportion to such group's aggregate unused E&P deficit as—"],[3,"(i) the product of—"],[4,"(I) such shareholder's group ownership percentage, multiplied by"],[4,"(II) the amount which would (but for this paragraph) be taken into account under section 951(a)(1) by reason of subsection (a) by such shareholder, bears to"],[3,"(ii) the aggregate amount determined under clause (i) with respect to all E&P net surplus shareholders in such group."],[0,"(F) Group ownership percentage"],[2,"For purposes of this paragraph, the term \"group ownership percentage\" means, with respect to any United States shareholder in any affiliated group, the percentage of the value of the stock of such United States shareholder which is held by other includible corporations in such affiliated group. Notwithstanding the preceding sentence, the group ownership percentage of the common parent of the affiliated group is 100 percent. Any term used in this subparagraph which is also used in section 1504 shall have the same meaning as when used in such section."],[0,"(c) Application of participation exemption to included income"],[0,"(1) In general"],[1,"In the case of a United States shareholder of a deferred foreign income corporation, there shall be allowed as a deduction for the taxable year in which an amount is included in the gross income of such United States shareholder under section 951(a)(1) by reason of this section an amount equal to the sum of—"],[2,"(A) the United States shareholder's 8 percent rate equivalent percentage of the excess (if any) of—"],[3,"(i) the amount so included as gross income, over"],[3,"(ii) the amount of such United States shareholder's aggregate foreign cash position, plus"],[2,"(B) the United States shareholder's 15.5 percent rate equivalent percentage of so much of the amount described in subparagraph (A)(ii) as does not exceed the amount described in subparagraph (A)(i)."],[0,"(2) 8 and 15.5 percent rate equivalent percentages"],[1,"For purposes of this subsection—"],[0,"(A) 8 percent rate equivalent percentage"]],"more":true,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/26/965?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"op":1,"head":"§965. Treatment of deferred foreign income upon transition to participation exemption system of taxation","tname":"Internal Revenue Code","by":{"0":"9, 10, 44, 46","1":"39, 39, 39, 39, 39, 39, 39"}},{"t":"usc","label":"26 U.S.C. § 1001","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/1001","subs":["(a)"],"by":{"0":"38","1":"39"}},{"t":"usc","label":"26 U.S.C. § 1256","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/1256","subs":["(a)","(b)"],"by":{"0":"52","1":"31, 31"}},{"t":"usc","label":"26 U.S.C. § 1362","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/1362","subs":["(a)","(d)(1)(B)"],"stext":{"head":"§1362. Election; revocation; termination","paras":[[0,"(a) Election"],[0,"(1) In general"],[1,"Except as provided in subsection (g), a small business corporation may elect, in accordance with the provisions of this section, to be an S corporation."],[0,"(2) All shareholders must consent to election"],[1,"An election under this subsection shall be valid only if all persons who are shareholders in such corporation on the day on which such election is made consent to such election."],[0,"(b) When made"],[0,"(1) In general"],[1,"An election under subsection (a) may be made by a small business corporation for any taxable year—"],[2,"(A) at any time during the preceding taxable year, or"],[2,"(B) at any time during the taxable year and on or before the 15th day of the 3d month of the taxable year."],[0,"(2) Certain elections made during 1st 2½ months treated as made for next taxable year"],[1,"If—"],[2,"(A) an election under subsection (a) is made for any taxable year during such year and on or before the 15th day of the 3d month of such year, but"],[2,"(B) either—"],[3,"(i) on 1 or more days in such taxable year before the day on which the election was made the corporation did not meet the requirements of subsection (b) of section 1361, or"],[3,"(ii) 1 or more of the persons who held stock in the corporation during such taxable year and before the election was made did not consent to the election,"],[1,"then such election shall be treated as made for the following taxable year."],[0,"(3) Election made after 1st 2½ months treated as made for following taxable year"],[1,"If—"],[2,"(A) a small business corporation makes an election under subsection (a) for any taxable year, and"],[2,"(B) such election is made after the 15th day of the 3d month of the taxable year and on or before the 15th day of the 3rd month of the following taxable year,"],[1,"then such election shall be treated as made for the following taxable year."],[0,"(4) Taxable years of 2½ months or less"],[1,"For purposes of this subsection, an election for a taxable year made not later than 2 months and 15 days after the first day of the taxable year shall be treated as timely made during such year."],[0,"(5) Authority to treat late elections, etc., as timely"],[1,"If—"],[2,"(A) an election under subsection (a) is made for any taxable year (determined without regard to paragraph (3)) after the date prescribed by this subsection for making such election for such taxable year or no such election is made for any taxable year, and"],[2,"(B) the Secretary determines that there was reasonable cause for the failure to timely make such election,"],[1,"the Secretary may treat such an election as timely made for such taxable year (and paragraph (3) shall not apply)."],[0,"(c) Years for which effective"],[0,"An election under subsection (a) shall be effective for the taxable year of the corporation for which it is made and for all succeeding taxable years of the corporation, until such election is terminated under subsection (d)."],[0,"(d) Termination"],[0,"(1) By revocation"],[0,"(A) In general"],[2,"An election under subsection (a) may be terminated by revocation."],[0,"(B) More than one-half of shares must consent to revocation"],[2,"An election may be revoked only if shareholders holding more than one-half of the shares of stock of the corporation on the day on which the revocation is made consent to the revocation."],[0,"(C) When effective"],[2,"Except as provided in subparagraph (D)—"],[3,"(i) a revocation made during the taxable year and on or before the 15th day of the 3d month thereof shall be effective on the 1st day of such taxable year, and"],[3,"(ii) a revocation made during the taxable year but after such 15th day shall be effective on the 1st day of the following taxable year."],[0,"(D) Revocation may specify prospective date"],[2,"If the revocation specifies a date for revocation which is on or after the day on which the revocation is made, the revocation shall be effective on and after the date so specified."],[0,"(2) By corporation ceasing to be small business corporation"],[0,"(A) In general"],[2,"An election under subsection (a) shall be terminated whenever (at any time on or after the 1st day of the 1st taxable year for which the corporation is an S corporation) such corporation ceases to be a small business corporation."],[0,"(B) When effective"],[2,"Any termination under this paragraph shall be effective on and after the date of cessation."],[0,"(3) Where passive investment income exceeds 25 percent of gross receipts for 3 consecutive taxable years and corporation has accumulated earnings and profits"],[0,"(A) Termination"],[0,"(i) In general"],[3,"An election under subsection (a) shall be terminated whenever the corporation—"],[4,"(I) has accumulated earnings and profits at the close of each of 3 consecutive taxable years, and"],[4,"(II) has gross receipts for each of such taxable years more than 25 percent of which are passive investment income."],[0,"(ii) When effective"],[3,"Any termination under this paragraph shall be effective on and after the first day of the first taxable year beginning after the third consecutive taxable year referred to in clause (i)."],[0,"(iii) Years taken into account"],[3,"A prior taxable year shall not be taken into account under clause (i) unless the corporation was an S corporation for such taxable year."],[0,"(B) Gross receipts from the sales of certain assets"],[2,"For purposes of this paragraph—"],[3,"(i) in the case of dispositions of capital assets (other than stock and securities), gross receipts from such dispositions shall be taken into account only to the extent of the capital gain net income therefrom, and"],[3,"(ii) in the case of sales or exchanges of stock or securities, gross receipts shall be taken into account only to the extent of the gains therefrom."],[0,"(C) Passive investment income defined"],[0,"(i) In general"],[3,"Except as otherwise provided in this subparagraph, the term \"passive investment income\" means gross receipts derived from royalties, rents, dividends, interest, and annuities."],[0,"(ii) Exception for interest on notes from sales of inventory"],[3,"The term \"passive investment income\" shall not include interest on any obligation acquired in the ordinary course of the corporation's trade or business from its sale of property described in section 1221(a)(1)."],[0,"(iii) Treatment of certain lending or finance companies"],[3,"If the S corporation meets the requirements of section 542(c)(6) for the taxable year, the term \"passive investment income\" shall not include gross receipts for the taxable year which are derived directly from the active and regular conduct of a lending or finance business (as defined in section 542(d)(1))."],[0,"(iv) Treatment of certain dividends"],[3,"If an S corporation holds stock in a C corporation meeting the requirements of section 1504(a)(2), the term \"passive investment income\" shall not include dividends from such C corporation to the extent such dividends are attributable to the earnings and profits of such C corporation derived from the active conduct of a trade or business."],[0,"(v) Exception for banks, etc."],[3,"In the case of a bank (as defined in section 581) or a depository institution holding company (as defined in section 3(w)(1) of the Federal Deposit Insurance Act (12 U.S.C. 1813(w)(1))), the term \"passive investment income\" shall not include—"],[4,"(I) interest income earned by such bank or company, or"],[4,"(II) dividends on assets required to be held by such bank or company, including stock in the Federal Reserve Bank, the Federal Home Loan Bank, or the Federal Agricultural Mortgage Bank or participation certificates issued by a Federal Intermediate Credit Bank."],[0,"(e) Treatment of S termination year"],[0,"(1) In general"],[1,"In the case of an S termination year, for purposes of this title—"],[0,"(A) S short year"],[2,"The portion of such year ending before the 1st day for which the termination is effective shall be treated as a short taxable year for which the corporation is an S corporation."],[0,"(B) C short year"],[2,"The portion of such year beginning on such 1st day shall be treated as a short taxable year for which the corporation is a C corporation."],[0,"(2) Pro rata allocation"],[1,"Except as provided in paragraph (3) and subparagraphs (C) and (D) of paragraph (6), the determination of which items are to be taken into account for each of the short taxable years referred to in paragraph (1) shall be made—"],[2,"(A) first by determining for the S termination year—"],[3,"(i) the amount of each of the items of income, loss, deduction, or credit described in section 1366(a)(1)(A), and"],[3,"(ii) the amount of the nonseparately computed income or loss, and"],[2,"(B) then by assigning an equal portion of each amount determined under subparagraph (A) to each day of the S termination year."],[0,"(3) Election to have items assigned to each short taxable year under normal tax accounting rules"],[0,"(A) In general"],[2,"A corporation may elect to have paragraph (2) not apply."],[0,"(B) Shareholders must consent to election"],[2,"An election under this subsection shall be valid only if all persons who are shareholders in the corporation at any time during the S short year and all persons who are shareholders in the corporation on the first day of the C short year consent to such election."],[0,"(4) S termination year"],[1,"For purposes of this subsection, the term \"S termination year\" means any taxable year of a corporation (determined without regard to this subsection) in which a termination of an election made under subsection (a) takes effect (other than on the 1st day thereof)."]],"more":true,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/26/1362?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"op":1,"head":"§1362. Election; revocation; termination","tname":"Internal Revenue Code","by":{"0":"51","1":"29, 29"}},{"t":"usc","label":"28 U.S.C. § 1254","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/1254","by":{"0":"4"}},{"t":"case","label":"Charles E. Clark, Eisner v. Macomber and Some Income Tax Problems","cite":"29 Yale L.J. 735","y":1920,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Yale%20L.J./29/735/","op":2,"by":{"0":"33"}},{"t":"case","label":"Bowers v. Kerbaugh-Empire Co.","cite":"271 U.S. 170","y":1926,"id":"scdb/1925-114","sc":1,"sum":"that the difference, resulting from the depreciation, between the amount borrowed and the amount repaid, in American money, was not taxable as \"income.\" P. 173. 300 Fed. 938, affirmed. ERROR to a judgment of the District Court recovered by the Company from the Collector in an action for money paid under protest as income tax. Assistant Attorney General Wilieblandt, with whom Solicitor General Mitchell and Messrs. …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"16, 18"}},{"t":"case","label":"Bufferd v. Commissioner","cite":"506 U.S. 523","y":1993,"id":"1992/91-7804","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"Subchapter S of the Internal Revenue Code seeks to eliminate tax disadvantages that might dissuade small businesses from adopting the corporate form and to lessen the tax burden on such businesses by means of a pass-through system under which corporate income, losses, deductions, and credits are attributed to individual shareholders in a manner akin to the tax treatment of partnerships. Petitioner Bufferd, a …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"29"}},{"t":"case","label":"Burnet v. Sanford & Brooks Co.","cite":"282 U.S. 359","y":1931,"id":"scdb/1930-115","sc":1,"sum":"(1) That, under the Revenue Act of 1918, the money received in 1920 was properly included by the Commissioner as part of the gross income for that year in ascertaining the taxable income for that year. P. 363. (2) That a judgment in effect eliminating this money from the 1920 computation upon the condition that the taxpayer amend the earlier returns by omitting therefrom the deductions of related expenditures, was …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"13, 35","2":"23, 24"}},{"t":"case","label":"Burnet v. Wells","cite":"289 U.S. 670","y":1933,"id":"scdb/1932-094","sc":1,"op":4,"sum":"1. Under § 219 (h) of the Revenue Acts of 1924 and 1926, where an irrevocable trust is established to pay for insurance on the settlor's life, collect the policy upon his death, and hold or apply the proceeds, under the trust, for the benefit of his dependents, income of the trust fund used by the trustee in paying the premiums, is taxable to the settlor as part of his own income.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"44"}},{"t":"case","label":"CFTC v. Schor","cite":"478 U.S. 833","y":1986,"id":"1985/85-621","sc":1,"sum":"Section 14 of the Commodity Exchange Act (CEA) provides that any person injured by a commodity broker's violation of the Act or regulations of the Commodity Futures Trading Commission (CFTC) may apply to the CFTC for an order directing the offender to pay reparations to the complainant and may enforce that order in federal district court. The CFTC promulgated a regulation that allows it in a reparations proceeding …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"29"}},{"t":"case","label":"City of Austin v. Reagan Nat’l Adver. of Austin, LLC","cite":"142 S. Ct. 1464","y":2022,"id":"2021/20-1029","sc":1,"sum":"The distinction between on-premises signs and off-premises signs in the City of Austin’s sign code is facially content neutral under the First Amendment.","sumSrc":"Holding (supremecourt.gov)","by":{"1":"49"}},{"t":"case","label":"Commissioner v. Obear-Nester Glass Co.","cite":"217 F.2d 56","y":1954,"court":"7th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/217/56/","op":1,"by":{"1":"39"}},{"t":"case","label":"Cottage Sav. Ass’n v. Commissioner","cite":"499 U.S. 554","y":1991,"id":"1990/89-1965","sc":1,"op":1,"ar":2,"sum":"Explaining that, properly understood, “the concept of realization is `founded on administrative convenience,' ” compared to the “ `cumbersome' ” process of “valuing assets on an annual basis to determine . . . appreciat[ion]”.","sumSrc":"opinion","by":{"1":"32, 39","2":"7"}},{"t":"case","label":"Eder v. Commissioner","cite":"138 F.2d 27","y":1943,"court":"2d Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/138/27/","op":1,"by":{"1":"26"}},{"t":"case","label":"Estate of Whitlock v. Commissioner: 59 T.C. 490 (1972), aff ’d in relevant part","cite":"494 F.2d 1297","y":1974,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/494/1297/","op":2,"by":{"1":"27, 46"}},{"t":"case","label":"Fernandez v. Wiener","cite":"326 U.S. 340","y":1945,"id":"scdb/1945-022","sc":1,"sum":"that the tax does not infringe any provision of the Federal Constitution. Pp. 342, 362. (1) The statute is a revenue measure enacted by Congress in the exercise of the federal power to lay and collect an excise. P. 351. (2) The tax does not violate the due process clause of the Fifth Amendment. Pp. 346, 357.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"47"}},{"t":"case","label":"Forsyth v. Woods","cite":"78 U.S. 484","y":1870,"id":"scdb/1870-118","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"Woods, assignee in bankruptcy of the firm of E. P. Tesson & Co. (which was composed of E. P. & E. M. Tesson) sued Forsyth in assumpsit to recover from him a balance in account.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"25"}},{"t":"case","label":"Garlock, Inc. v. Commissioner","cite":"489 F.2d 197","y":1973,"court":"2d. Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/489/197/","op":2,"by":{"1":"26, 27, 40"}},{"t":"case","label":"Helvering v. Clifford","cite":"309 U.S. 331","y":1940,"id":"scdb/1939-115","sc":1,"op":3,"sum":"Mr„ Warner W. Gardner, with wdiom Solicitor General Jackson, Assistant Attorney General Clark, and Messrs. Bewail Key, L. TV. Post, and Richard H. Demuth were on the brief, for petitioner.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"12"}},{"t":"case","label":"Helvering v. Estate of Enright","cite":"312 U.S. 636","y":1941,"id":"scdb/1940-102","sc":1,"sum":"Section 42 of the Revenue Act of 1934 permits the inclusion, as accruable items, in a decedent's gross income for the period ending with his death, of his share of the profits earned, but not yet received, by a partnership, although both the decedent and his firm kept their accounts and made their income tax reports on a calendar year cash receipts and disbursements basis. P. 640. So held of a deceased member of a …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"30"}},{"t":"case","label":"Helvering v. National Grocery Co.","cite":"304 U.S. 282","y":1938,"id":"scdb/1937-083","sc":1,"op":6,"sum":"(1) The Act does not violate the Tenth Amendment by interfering with the right of the corporation to declare or withhold dividends. It merely lays the tax upon corporations that use their powers to prevent imposition upon their stockholders of the federt surtaxes. P. 286. (2) The Act is not unconstitutional as imposing, not a tax ujion income, but a penalty to force distribution of corporate earnings in order to …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"23, 26"}},{"t":"case","label":"Knowlton v. Moore","cite":"178 U.S. 41","y":1900,"id":"scdb/1899-043","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"The plaintiffs in error were the executors of the will of Edwin F. Knowiton, of Brooklyn, New York. The defendant in error was the United States Collector of Internal Revenue for the First Collection District for the State of New York. Mr. Knowiton died at Brooklyn in October, 1898, and his will was duly proved. Under the portion of the act of Congress of June 13, 1898, which is printed at length in a note to the …","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"47"}},{"t":"case","label":"Moore v. Harper","cite":"143 S. Ct. 2065","y":2023,"id":"2022/21-1271","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"The Federal Elections Clause does not vest exclusive and independent authority in state legislatures to set the rules regarding federal elections and therefore did not bar the North Carolina Supreme Court from reviewing the North Carolina Legislature’s congressional districting plans for compliance with North Carolina law.","sumSrc":"Holding (supremecourt.gov)","by":{"1":"22"}},{"t":"case","label":"PPL Corp. v. Commissioner","cite":"569 U.S. 329","y":2013,"id":"2012/12-43","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that courts should use a common-sense, substance-based approach instead of a formalistic approach when considering the effect of a foreign tax.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"41, 43"}},{"t":"case","label":"Pollock v. Farmers’ Loan & Trust Co.","cite":"158 U.S. 601","y":1895,"id":"scdb/1894-236","sc":1,"op":10,"sum":"Hylton v. United States, 3 Dali. 171, further considered, and, in view of the '' historical evidence cited, shown to have only decided that the' tax on carriages involved was an excise, and was therefore an indirect tax.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"3, 19, 12, 14","2":"5, 9, 12, 14, 24-26"}},{"t":"case","label":"Seila Law LLC v. CFPB","cite":"140 S. Ct. 2183","y":2020,"id":"2019/19-7","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the vesting of substantial executive authority in the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau, an independent agency led by a single director, violates the separation of powers, but the statutory provision creating that violation, 12 U.S.C. § 5491(c)(3) is severable from the Dodd-Frank Act.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"22"}},{"t":"case","label":"Spring City Foundry Co. v. Commissioner","cite":"292 U.S. 182","y":1934,"id":"scdb/1933-118","sc":1,"sum":"1. Where accounts and income tax returns are on the accrual basis, a debt owing the taxpayer for goods sold in the tax year is returnable as gross income of that year even though ascertained in that year to be partly worthless. Art. 35 of Regs. 45, under Revenue Act of 1918, construed.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"30"}},{"t":"case","label":"Springer v. United States","cite":"102 U.S. 586","y":1881,"id":"scdb/1880-080","sc":1,"op":4,"ar":2,"sum":"In June, 1866, the deputy assessor of internal revenue -for the proper district in Illinois delivered to William M. Springer a notice in writing, with certain accompanying forms, requiring himwithin ten days to make out and réturn, according to those forms, a list of his income, gains, and profits for the year 1865. In compliance therewith, Springer made out the neces-' sary statement, dated June 21, 1866, and …","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"3","2":"6"}},{"t":"case","label":"Stanton v. Baltic Mining Co.","cite":"240 U.S. 103","y":1916,"id":"scdb/1915-080","sc":1,"op":7,"sum":"Brushaber v. Un. Pac. R. R., ante, p. 1, followed, to effect that the District Court has jurisdiction of an action by a stockholder against the corporation to enjoin' it from voluntarily paying the tax under the Income Tax Law of 1913 on the ground of its unconstitutionality.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"4, 14, 18"}},{"t":"case","label":"State ex rel. Bolens v. Frear","cite":"134 N.W. 673","y":1912,"court":"Wis.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/N.W./134/673/","by":{"1":"20"}},{"t":"case","label":"Thomas v. United States","cite":"192 U.S. 363","y":1904,"id":"scdb/1903-180","sc":1,"sum":"The words duties, imposts and excises were used comprehensively in the Constitution to cover customs and excise duties imposed on importation^ consumption, manufacture and sale of certain commodities, privileges, particular business transactions, vocations and the like. The stamp duty on sales of shares of stock in corporations imposed by the War Revenue Act of 1898, 30 Stat. 448, falls within that category and was …","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"47","2":"22"}},{"t":"case","label":"Trefry v. Putnam","cite":"116 N.E. 904","y":1917,"court":"Mass.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/N.E./116/904/","sum":"Explaining that “[c]apital is a static conception independent of time”.","sumSrc":"brief:1","by":{"1":"15, 19","2":"12, 13"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Basye","cite":"410 U.S. 441","y":1973,"id":"1972/71-1022","sc":1,"op":3,"sum":"The retirement fund payments, notwithstanding the fact that they were contributed directly to the trust, were compensation for services that Permanente rendered under the medical-service agreement and should have been reported as income to Permanente; and the individual partners should have included their shares of that income in their individual returns, since the existence of conditions upon the actual receipt by …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"29"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Burke","cite":"504 U.S. 229","y":1992,"id":"1991/91-42","sc":1,"sum":"Backpay awards in settlement of Title VII claims are not excludable from gross income under §104(a)(2).","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"12"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Carlton","cite":"512 U.S. 26","y":1994,"id":"1993/92-1941","sc":1,"op":1,"ar":1,"sum":"The 1987 amendment's retroactive application to Carlton's 1986 transactions does not violate due process. Under the applicable standard, a tax statute's retroactive application must be supported by a legitimate legislative purpose furthered by rational means. See, e. g., Pension Benefit GuarantyCorporationv. R. A. Gray & Co., 467 U. S. 717, 729-730. Here, Congress' purpose in enacting the 1987 amendment was neither …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"43"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Clintwood Elkhorn Mining Co.","cite":"553 U.S. 1","y":2008,"id":"2007/07-308","sc":1,"sum":"Jr., the Court refused to pay Clintwood refunds for the unconstitutionally levied taxes. The Court stated that the remedy for taxes imposed in violation of the Export Clause is the same as for any other unlawful tax: filing a timely administrative refund claim.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"49"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Galletti","cite":"541 U.S. 114","y":2004,"id":"2003/02-1389","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the IRS' assessment against the partnership was sufficient to extend the statute of limitations by 10 years to collect the money from the partners themselves.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"25"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Hansen","cite":"143 S. Ct. 1932","y":2023,"id":"2022/22-179","sc":1,"sum":"Title 8 U. S. C. §1324(a)(1)(A)(iv)—which criminalizes “encouraging or inducing” illegal immigration—forbids only the purposeful solicitation and facilitation of specific acts known to violate federal law and is not unconstitutionally overbroad.","sumSrc":"Holding (supremecourt.gov)","by":{"1":"45"}},{"t":"case","label":"Walker v. Wait","cite":"50 Vt. 668","y":1878,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Vt./50/668/","by":{"1":"25"}},{"t":"const","label":"Amendment V","clauses":[["due-process-5","Due Process Clause (Fifth Amendment)"]],"text":"No person shall be held to answer for a capital, or otherwise infamous crime, unless on a presentment or indictment of a Grand Jury, except in cases arising in the land or naval forces, or in the Militia, when in actual service in time of War or public danger; nor shall any person be subject for the same offence to be twice put in jeopardy of life or limb; nor shall be compelled in any criminal case to be a witness against himself, nor be deprived of life, liberty, or property, without due process of law; nor shall private property be taken for public use, without just compensation.","by":{"1":"7, 44"}},{"t":"const","label":"Amendment XVI, Section 116","clauses":[["sixteenth-amendment","Sixteenth Amendment (Income Tax)"]],"text":"The Congress shall have power to lay and collect taxes on incomes, from whatever source derived, without apportionment among the several States, and without regard to any census or enumeration.","by":{"1":"16"}},{"t":"usc","label":"26 U.S.C. § 1","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/1","stext":{"head":"§1. Tax imposed","paras":[[0,"(a) Married individuals filing joint returns and surviving spouses"],[0,"There is hereby imposed on the taxable income of—"],[1,"(1) every married individual (as defined in section 7703) who makes a single return jointly with his spouse under section 6013, and"],[1,"(2) every surviving spouse (as defined in section 2(a)),"],[0,"a tax determined in accordance with the following table:"],[0,"(b) Heads of households"],[0,"There is hereby imposed on the taxable income of every head of a household (as defined in section 2(b)) a tax determined in accordance with the following table:"],[0,"(c) Unmarried individuals (other than surviving spouses and heads of households)"],[0,"There is hereby imposed on the taxable income of every individual (other than a surviving spouse as defined in section 2(a) or the head of a household as defined in section 2(b)) who is not a married individual (as defined in section 7703) a tax determined in accordance with the following table:"],[0,"(d) Married individuals filing separate returns"],[0,"There is hereby imposed on the taxable income of every married individual (as defined in section 7703) who does not make a single return jointly with his spouse under section 6013, a tax determined in accordance with the following table:"],[0,"(e) Estates and trusts"],[0,"There is hereby imposed on the taxable income of—"],[1,"(1) every estate, and"],[1,"(2) every trust,"],[0,"taxable under this subsection a tax determined in accordance with the following table:"],[0,"(f) Phaseout of marriage penalty in 15-percent bracket; adjustments in tax tables so that inflation will not result in tax increases"],[0,"(1) In general"],[1,"Not later than December 15 of 1993, and each subsequent calendar year, the Secretary shall prescribe tables which shall apply in lieu of the tables contained in subsections (a), (b), (c), (d), and (e) with respect to taxable years beginning in the succeeding calendar year."],[0,"(2) Method of prescribing tables"],[1,"The table which under paragraph (1) is to apply in lieu of the table contained in subsection (a), (b), (c), (d), or (e), as the case may be, with respect to taxable years beginning in any calendar year shall be prescribed—"],[2,"(A) except as provided in paragraph (8), by increasing the minimum and maximum dollar amounts for each bracket for which a tax is imposed under such table by the cost-of-living adjustment for such calendar year, determined—"],[3,"(i) except as provided in clause (ii), by substituting \"1992\" for \"2016\" in paragraph (3)(A)(ii), and"],[3,"(ii) in the case of adjustments to the dollar amounts at which the 36 percent rate bracket begins or at which the 39.6 percent rate bracket begins, by substituting \"1993\" for \"2016\" in paragraph (3)(A)(ii),"],[2,"(B) by not changing the rate applicable to any rate bracket as adjusted under subparagraph (A), and"],[2,"(C) by adjusting the amounts setting forth the tax to the extent necessary to reflect the adjustments in the rate brackets."],[0,"(3) Cost-of-living adjustment"],[1,"For purposes of this subsection—"],[0,"(A) In general"],[2,"The cost-of-living adjustment for any calendar year is the percentage (if any) by which—"],[3,"(i) the C-CPI-U for the preceding calendar year, exceeds"],[3,"(ii) the CPI for calendar year 2016, multiplied by the amount determined under subparagraph (B)."],[0,"(B) Amount determined"],[2,"The amount determined under this clause is the amount obtained by dividing—"],[3,"(i) the C-CPI-U for calendar year 2016, by"],[3,"(ii) the CPI for calendar year 2016."],[0,"(C) Special rule for adjustments with a base year after 2016"],[2,"For purposes of any provision of this title which provides for the substitution of a year after 2016 for \"2016\" in subparagraph (A)(ii), subparagraph (A) shall be applied by substituting \"the C-CPI-U for calendar year 2016\" for \"the CPI for calendar year 2016\" and all that follows in clause (ii) thereof."],[0,"(4) CPI for any calendar year"],[1,"For purposes of paragraph (3), the CPI for any calendar year is the average of the Consumer Price Index as of the close of the 12-month period ending on August 31 of such calendar year."],[0,"(5) Consumer Price Index"],[1,"For purposes of paragraph (4), the term \"Consumer Price Index\" means the last Consumer Price Index for all-urban consumers published by the Department of Labor. For purposes of the preceding sentence, the revision of the Consumer Price Index which is most consistent with the Consumer Price Index for calendar year 1986 shall be used."],[0,"(6) C-CPI-U"],[1,"For purposes of this subsection—"],[0,"(A) In general"],[2,"The term \"C-CPI-U\" means the Chained Consumer Price Index for All Urban Consumers (as published by the Bureau of Labor Statistics of the Department of Labor). The values of the Chained Consumer Price Index for All Urban Consumers taken into account for purposes of determining the cost-of-living adjustment for any calendar year under this subsection shall be the latest values so published as of the date on which such Bureau publishes the initial value of the Chained Consumer Price Index for All Urban Consumers for the month of August for the preceding calendar year."],[0,"(B) Determination for calendar year"],[2,"The C-CPI-U for any calendar year is the average of the C-CPI-U as of the close of the 12-month period ending on August 31 of such calendar year."],[0,"(7) Rounding"],[0,"(A) In general"],[2,"If any increase determined under paragraph (2)(A), section 63(c)(4), section 68(b)(2) or section 151(d)(4) is not a multiple of $50, such increase shall be rounded to the next lowest multiple of $50."],[0,"(B) Table for married individuals filing separately"],[2,"In the case of a married individual filing a separate return, subparagraph (A) (other than with respect to sections 63(c)(4) and 151(d)(4)(A)) shall be applied by substituting \"$25\" for \"$50\" each place it appears."],[0,"(8) Elimination of marriage penalty in 15-percent bracket"],[1,"With respect to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2003, in prescribing the tables under paragraph (1)—"],[2,"(A) the maximum taxable income in the 15-percent rate bracket in the table contained in subsection (a) (and the minimum taxable income in the next higher taxable income bracket in such table) shall be 200 percent of the maximum taxable income in the 15-percent rate bracket in the table contained in subsection (c) (after any other adjustment under this subsection), and"],[2,"(B) the comparable taxable income amounts in the table contained in subsection (d) shall be ½ of the amounts determined under subparagraph (A)."],[0,"(g) Certain unearned income of children taxed as if parent's income"],[0,"(1) In general"],[1,"In the case of any child to whom this subsection applies, the tax imposed by this section shall be equal to the greater of—"],[2,"(A) the tax imposed by this section without regard to this subsection, or"],[2,"(B) the sum of—"],[3,"(i) the tax which would be imposed by this section if the taxable income of such child for the taxable year were reduced by the net unearned income of such child, plus"],[3,"(ii) such child's share of the allocable parental tax."],[0,"(2) Child to whom subsection applies"],[1,"This subsection shall apply to any child for any taxable year if—"],[2,"(A) such child—"],[3,"(i) has not attained age 18 before the close of the taxable year, or"],[3,"(ii)(I) has attained age 18 before the close of the taxable year and meets the age requirements of section 152(c)(3) (determined without regard to subparagraph (B) thereof), and"],[3,"(II) whose earned income (as defined in section 911(d)(2)) for such taxable year does not exceed one-half of the amount of the individual's support (within the meaning of section 152(c)(1)(D) after the application of section 152(f)(5) (without regard to subparagraph (A) thereof)) for such taxable year,"],[2,"(B) either parent of such child is alive at the close of the taxable year, and"],[2,"(C) such child does not file a joint return for the taxable year."],[0,"(3) Allocable parental tax"],[1,"For purposes of this subsection—"],[0,"(A) In general"],[2,"The term \"allocable parental tax\" means the excess of—"],[3,"(i) the tax which would be imposed by this section on the parent's taxable income if such income included the net unearned income of all children of the parent to whom this subsection applies, over"],[3,"(ii) the tax imposed by this section on the parent without regard to this subsection."],[2,"For purposes of clause (i), net unearned income of all children of the parent shall not be taken into account in computing any exclusion, deduction, or credit of the parent."],[0,"(B) Child's share"],[2,"A child's share of any allocable parental tax of a parent shall be equal to an amount which bears the same ratio to the total allocable parental tax as the child's net unearned income bears to the aggregate net unearned income of all children of such parent to whom this subsection applies."],[0,"(C) Special rule where parent has different taxable year"],[2,"Except as provided in regulations, if the parent does not have the same taxable year as the child, the allocable parental tax shall be determined on the basis of the taxable year of the parent ending in the child's taxable year."],[0,"(4) Net unearned income"],[1,"For purposes of this subsection—"],[0,"(A) In general"],[2,"The term \"net unearned income\" means the excess of—"],[3,"(i) the portion of the adjusted gross income for the taxable year which is not attributable to earned income (as defined in section 911(d)(2)), over"],[3,"(ii) the sum of—"]],"more":true,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/26/1?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"op":4,"head":"§1. Tax imposed","tname":"Internal Revenue Code","by":{"1":"5"}},{"t":"usc","label":"26 U.S.C. § 11","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/11","subs":["(b)(1)(2012)"],"stext":{"head":"§11. Tax imposed","paras":[[0,"(a) Corporations in general"],[0,"A tax is hereby imposed for each taxable year on the taxable income of every corporation."],[0,"(b) Amount of tax"],[0,"The amount of the tax imposed by subsection (a) shall be 21 percent of taxable income."],[0,"(c) Exceptions"],[0,"Subsection (a) shall not apply to a corporation subject to a tax imposed by—"],[1,"(1) section 594 (relating to mutual savings banks conducting life insurance business),"],[1,"(2) subchapter L (sec. 801 and following, relating to insurance companies), or"],[1,"(3) subchapter M (sec. 851 and following, relating to regulated investment companies and real estate investment trusts)."],[0,"(d) Foreign corporations"],[0,"In the case of a foreign corporation, the taxes imposed by subsection (a) and section 55 shall apply only as provided by section 882."]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/26/11?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"op":1,"head":"§11. Tax imposed","tname":"Internal Revenue Code","by":{"1":"5, 5"}},{"t":"usc","label":"26 U.S.C. § 446","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/446","subs":["(c)(2)"],"stext":{"head":"§446. General rule for methods of accounting","paras":[[0,"(a) General rule"],[0,"Taxable income shall be computed under the method of accounting on the basis of which the taxpayer regularly computes his income in keeping his books."],[0,"(b) Exceptions"],[0,"If no method of accounting has been regularly used by the taxpayer, or if the method used does not clearly reflect income, the computation of taxable income shall be made under such method as, in the opinion of the Secretary, does clearly reflect income."],[0,"(c) Permissible methods"],[0,"Subject to the provisions of subsections (a) and (b), a taxpayer may compute taxable income under any of the following methods of accounting—"],[1,"(1) the cash receipts and disbursements method;"],[1,"(2) an accrual method;"],[1,"(3) any other method permitted by this chapter; or"],[1,"(4) any combination of the foregoing methods permitted under regulations prescribed by the Secretary."],[0,"(d) Taxpayer engaged in more than one business"],[0,"A taxpayer engaged in more than one trade or business may, in computing taxable income, use a different method of accounting for each trade or business."],[0,"(e) Requirement respecting change of accounting method"],[0,"Except as otherwise expressly provided in this chapter, a taxpayer who changes the method of accounting on the basis of which he regularly computes his income in keeping his books shall, before computing his taxable income under the new method, secure the consent of the Secretary."],[0,"(f) Failure to request change of method of accounting"],[0,"If the taxpayer does not file with the Secretary a request to change the method of accounting, the absence of the consent of the Secretary to a change in the method of accounting shall not be taken into account—"],[1,"(1) to prevent the imposition of any penalty, or the addition of any amount to tax, under this title, or"],[1,"(2) to diminish the amount of such penalty or addition to tax."]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/26/446?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"op":1,"head":"§446. General rule for methods of accounting","tname":"Internal Revenue Code","by":{"1":"30"}},{"t":"usc","label":"26 U.S.C. § 448","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/448","subs":["(a)"],"by":{"1":"30"}},{"t":"usc","label":"26 U.S.C. § 467","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/467","subs":["(a)"],"by":{"1":"31"}},{"t":"usc","label":"26 U.S.C. § 1259","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/1259","by":{"1":"31"}},{"t":"usc","label":"26 U.S.C. § 1272","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/1272","subs":["(a)(1)"],"by":{"1":"31"}},{"t":"usc","label":"26 U.S.C. § 1296","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/1296","subs":["(a)"],"by":{"1":"31"}},{"t":"usc","label":"26 U.S.C. § 1366","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/1366","subs":["(a)","(b)"],"by":{"1":"29, 29"}},{"t":"law","label":"Pub. L. No. 87-834","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/statute/76/1006","by":{"1":"4, 26"}},{"t":"law","label":"Tax Cuts and Jobs Act, Pub. L. No. 115-97","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/plaw/115/public/97","by":{"1":"5"}},{"t":"reg","label":"26 C.F.R. § 301.7701-3","url":"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-26/section-301.7701-3","subs":["(a)"],"by":{"1":"29"}},{"t":"case","label":"Collector v. Hubbard","cite":"79 U.S. 1","y":1871,"id":"scdb/1870-102","sc":1,"op":5,"ar":1,"sum":"The 117th section of the Internal Revenue Act of June 30th, 1864, which laid what was known as* the income tax, after providiug for the collection of an income tax from certain classes of companies specified, and enacting that “in estimating the annual gains, profits, or income of any person,” revenue from such and such sources “shall be included and assessed as part of the income of such person,” proceeds:","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"2":"5, 8, 9, 15"}},{"t":"case","label":"Moline Properties, Inc. v. Comm’r","cite":"319 U.S. 436","y":1943,"id":"scdb/1942-081","sc":1,"sum":"1. Upon the facts of this case, held that, for the purpose of the federal income tax, gains from sales (in 1935 and 1936) by a corporation of its property, although the corporation was owned wholly by an individual stockholder, could not be treated as income taxable to the individual rather than to the corporation.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"2":"19"}},{"t":"case","label":"Nathel v. Comm’r","cite":"615 F.3d 83","y":2010,"court":"2d Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/615/83/","by":{"2":"8"}},{"t":"case","label":"Towne v. Eisner","cite":"245 U.S. 418","y":1918,"id":"scdb/1917-208","sc":1,"sum":"In an action to recover back money collected and retained by the Government, over plaintiff’s protest, as a tax on incomeunder the Income Tax Law of 1913, plaintiff alleged that that upon which the tax was levied, a Stock dividend based on accumulated profits, was not “income” within the true intent of the statute, and that if the statute so intended it was so far unconstitutional, because in the Sixteenth …","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"2":"3, 18"}},{"t":"case","label":"Yee v. Escondido","cite":"503 U.S. 519","y":1992,"id":"1991/90-1947","sc":1,"sum":"The Fifth Amendment's Takings Clause generally requires just compensation where the government authorizes a physical occupation of property. But where the government merely regulates the property's use, compensation is required only if considerations such as the regulation's purpose or the extent to which it deprives the owner of the property's economic use suggests that the regulation has unfairly singled out the …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"2":"24"}},{"t":"usc","label":"26 U.S.C. § 956","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/956","by":{"2":"25"}}]};